Object.assign(ROOT_STORIES.shared, {
  aqu: ["aqua(水)来自印欧语 *h₂ekʷ-(水),同根有哥特语 aƕa(河)、古英语 ēa(河,残留在地名 Eaton)、德语 Ache、Aue(河畔草地)、古挪威语 á。罗马人的 aqua 是水,尤其是引来的水:Aqua Appia(前 312 年第一条引水渠)、Aqua Claudia;aquae-ductus 引水道(aqueduct),aquarius 水瓶座、送水人(aquarium 1830 年代),aquaticus 水生的,aquosus 多水的,Aquitania 水乡,aquavit/eau-de-vie 是 aqua vitae\"生命之水\"(蒸馏酒),法语 eau、意大利语 acqua、西班牙语 agua,aquamarine 海水色宝石,gouache 是意大利语 guazzo 水洼,ewer 水壶是 aquarius 经法语,sewer 下水道是 ex-aquare\"排水\"!",
    "aqua (water) comes from PIE *h₂ekʷ- (water), shared with Gothic aƕa (a river), Old English ēa (a river, surviving in place-names like Eaton), German Ache, Aue (river-meadow), Old Norse á. Roman aqua was water, above all water brought by channel: Aqua Appia (312 BC, the first aqueduct), Aqua Claudia; aquae-ductus a water-leading (aqueduct), aquarius the water-carrier and the zodiac sign (aquarium 1830s), aquaticus, aquosus watery, Aquitania the land of waters, aquavit and eau-de-vie aqua vitae, the water of life — spirits, French eau, Italian acqua, Spanish agua, aquamarine sea-water blue, gouache Italian guazzo, a puddle, ewer aquarius through French, sewer ex-aquare, to drain!"],
  carn: ["caro(肉,属格 carnis)来自印欧语 *(s)ker-(切)——肉是\"切下的份\";同根有希腊语 keirein(剪)、英语 shear、share、德语 scheren、翁布里亚语 karu(份)。罗马人 caro 是肉、也是肉身;carnalis 肉体的(carnal),carni-vorus 食肉的,carnificina 屠夫的活——刑场,carnifex 刽子手(\"做肉的\"),in-carnatio 成肉身(道成肉身),carnival 是 carne-levare\"去肉\"——大斋前的狂欢(1540 年代经意大利语 carnevale),carnation 是肉色的花,carrion 腐肉(caronia),carnage 屠杀,charnel 藏骨堂,chair 法语肉,carne 意大利语/西班牙语肉,carnitas,reincarnation 转世。",
    "caro (flesh, genitive carnis) comes from PIE *(s)ker- (to cut) — meat is the portion cut off; shared with Greek keirein (to shear), English shear and share, German scheren, Umbrian karu (a portion). Roman caro was meat and the body; carnalis of the flesh (carnal), carni-vorus flesh-eating, carnificina the butcher's work — the place of execution, carnifex the executioner (the flesh-maker), in-carnatio the taking of flesh (the Incarnation), carnival carne-levare, the putting away of meat — the feast before Lent (1540s via Italian carnevale), carnation the flesh-coloured flower, carrion caronia, carnage, charnel-house, French chair, Italian and Spanish carne, reincarnation."],
  bell: ["bellum(战争)是古拉丁语 duellum 的变形(du- 变 b-,如 duis→bis);duellum 的词源不明——古人联想 duo(两),因此 duel(决斗)在中世纪拉丁语里被重新理解为\"二人之战\"(1590 年代)。罗马的战神庙 Bellona 是战争女神,templum Bellonae 前的 columna bellica 是宣战掷矛之柱;bellicus 战争的(bellicose 好战),belli-ger 打仗的(belligerent 1570 年代——交战方),rebellare 再举兵(rebel 是被征服者再反,rebellion),ante-bellum 战前(美国内战前),casus belli 战争借口,Bellona;bellus(美)是另一个词(bonus 的指小)。",
    "bellum (war) is a reshaping of Old Latin duellum (du- became b-, as duis became bis); duellum's origin is unknown — the ancients linked it to duo (two), so duel was re-read in medieval Latin as a fight of two (1590s). Bellona was Rome's war-goddess; before her temple stood the columna bellica, over which a spear was hurled to declare war; bellicus of war (bellicose), belli-ger war-waging (belligerent 1570s — a party at war), rebellare to take up arms again (a rebel was a conquered people rising again; rebellion), ante-bellum before the war (America's Civil War), casus belli; bellus (pretty) is another word, the diminutive of bonus."],
  mil: ["miles(士兵,属格 militis)词源不明——可能与 mille(千)有关(\"千人队的一员\")或借自伊特鲁里亚语。罗马公民兵 miles 服役 militia(兵役、军事),militaris 军人的(military 1580 年代),militare 当兵(militate 是\"作战反对\"——不利于),militans 战斗中的(militant;ecclesia militans 是尘世战斗的教会),militia 在英语先指军务,1690 年代指民兵,soldier 是另一个词(solidus 金币——领饷者),miliziano,militaire,militar。",
    "miles (a soldier, genitive militis) is of unknown origin — perhaps tied to mille (a thousand; one of the thousand-strong unit) or Etruscan. The Roman citizen-soldier miles served militia (military service, warfare), militaris of soldiers (military 1580s), militare to serve (militate — to fight against, to tell against), militans fighting (militant; the ecclesia militans is the Church at war on earth), militia meant warfare in English before the 1690s made it a citizen army; soldier is a different word (solidus, the gold coin — one paid in it), militaire, militar."],
  brev: ["brevis(短)来自印欧语 *mreǵʰ-u-(短),同根有希腊语 brachys(短——brachy-,brachylogy)、英语 merry(古义\"短暂的\"→愉快!)、mirth、德语 murgen?否、阿维斯陀语 mərəzu(短)。罗马人 brevis 短;breviarium 摘要——教会的每日祷文书(breviary),brevitas 简短(brevity;哈姆雷特\"简洁是智慧的灵魂\"),ab-breviare 缩短(abbreviate),brief 是 breve 经法语——教皇的简短敕书(brief 1300 年代),后指律师摘要、内裤(1930 年代),breve 音乐的二全音符(曾是最短的音符!),brevet 委任状,brevier 小号字,semi-breve 全音符,briefing 简报 1910 年代。",
    "brevis (short) comes from PIE *mreǵʰ-u- (short), shared with Greek brachys (short — brachy-), English merry (once short-lasting → pleasant!) and mirth, Avestan mərəzu. Roman brevis is short; breviarium a summary — the Church's daily office-book (breviary), brevitas (brevity; Hamlet's soul of wit), ab-breviare to shorten (abbreviate), brief breve through French — a short papal letter (1300s), later a lawyer's summary and underwear (1930s), breve the double whole-note in music (once the shortest note!), brevet a commission, brevier a small type size, semi-breve, briefing 1910s."],
  vulg: ["vulgus(民众、大众)词源不明,可能与梵语 vargaḥ(群)有关。罗马人的 vulgus 是无名的大众(与 populus 有组织的公民相对);vulgaris 大众的、普通的(vulgar 先是\"通俗的\",18 世纪才转贬),Vulgata 是哲罗姆 4 世纪末的\"通行本\"拉丁圣经(武加大译本),vulgare 公之于众(divulge 1460 年代),vulgate 通行本,vulgarity 1770 年代,vulgo 意大利语\"俗称\",Vulgar Latin 通俗拉丁语(罗曼语的母亲),林奈的 vulgaris 是物种名\"常见的\"。",
    "vulgus (the common people) is of unknown origin, possibly tied to Sanskrit vargaḥ (a group). The Roman vulgus was the nameless crowd (as against the organised populus); vulgaris of the people, common (vulgar meant ordinary until the 18th century made it coarse), the Vulgata Jerome's late 4th-century \"common\" Latin Bible (the Vulgate), vulgare to make public (divulge 1460s), vulgarity 1770s, Italian vulgo commonly called, Vulgar Latin the people's speech that became the Romance languages, Linnaeus' vulgaris the species-name for common."],
  quer: ["queri(抱怨,分词 questus)来自印欧语 *kʷes-(喘息、叹息)存疑,同根的可能有梵语 śvasiti(呼吸、叹)。罗马人 queri 是哀叹、也是法律上的申诉;querela 控诉(quarrel 先指起诉,1300 年代经法语 querele——后指争吵),querimonia 抱怨,querulus 爱抱怨的(querulous 1400 年代),conqueri 一起抱怨,quejarse 西班牙语抱怨,cry 无关;quaerere(寻找)是另一个词(question、query)。",
    "queri (to complain, participle questus) may come from PIE *kʷes- (to pant, sigh), with Sanskrit śvasiti (breathes, sighs) a possible cousin. Roman queri was lament and a legal complaint; querela a complaint (quarrel meant a lawsuit in the 1300s through French querele, later a dispute), querimonia a grievance, querulus complaining (querulous 1400s), conqueri to complain together, Spanish quejarse; quaerere (to seek) is a different word (question, query)."],
  tac: ["tacere(沉默)来自印欧语 *tak-(安静),同根有哥特语 þahan(沉默)、古高地德语 dagēn、德语 无。罗马人 tacere 不说话;tacitus 默默的(tacit 1600 年代——tacit consent 默示同意;历史家 Tacitus 就叫\"沉默者\"),taciturnus 寡言(taciturn),taciturnitas,reticere 保持缄默(reticent 1830 年代),tacet 是乐谱的\"此处不奏\",tacere 意大利语,callar 是西班牙语另一个词(calare),tais-toi 法语\"闭嘴\"。",
    "tacere (to be silent) comes from PIE *tak- (to be quiet), shared with Gothic þahan (to be silent), Old High German dagēn. Roman tacere said nothing; tacitus unspoken (tacit 1600s — tacit consent; the historian Tacitus is the Silent One), taciturnus of few words (taciturn), reticere to keep silent (reticent 1830s), tacet the score's mark for silence, Italian tacere, French tais-toi be quiet; Spanish callar is a different word (calare)."],
  sept: ["septem(七)是印欧语 *septḿ̥:希腊语 hepta、梵语 sapta、英语 seven、德语 sieben、俄语 sem'。罗马历的 September 是第七个月(旧历三月开年);septimana 七天——一周(法语 semaine、西班牙语 semana、意大利语 settimana:基督教传入前罗马用八日 nundinae 市集周!),septuaginta 七十——七十士译本(传说七十二位译者),septen-trio 北方(北斗七星\"七头牛\"),septet 七重奏,septennial 七年一次,septic 与此无关(sēptikos 腐),Septimius 第七子,hebdomad 是希腊线的七。",
    "septem (seven) is Indo-European *septḿ̥: Greek hepta, Sanskrit sapta, English seven, German sieben, Russian sem'. September was the seventh month of the old March-first calendar; septimana seven days — a week (French semaine, Spanish semana, Italian settimana: before Christianity Rome kept an eight-day market cycle, the nundinae!), septuaginta seventy — the Septuagint (seventy-two legendary translators), septen-trio the north (the seven plough-oxen of the Great Bear), septet, septennial, Septimius a seventh son; septic is unrelated (sēptikos, rotting), and hebdomad is the Greek line's seven."],
  mur: ["murus(墙)来自古拉丁语 moiros,印欧语 *mei-(固定、建)存疑,同根有 munire(筑防)、moenia(城墙)。罗马的 murus 是城墙(塞尔维乌斯墙、奥勒良墙);muralis 墙的(mural 壁画 1850 年代),inter-murale 城墙间的,im-murare 砌进墙里(immure),muraille 法语城墙,muro 意大利语/西班牙语,Mauer 德语墙是从 murus 借的(柏林墙 Berliner Mauer!),mure 法语墙,munire 筑防→ munitions 军需、ammunition,muniment 契据(\"防御\"权利的文件)。",
    "murus (a wall) is Old Latin moiros, PIE *mei- (to fix, build) possible, kin to munire (to fortify) and moenia (city walls). Roman murus was the city wall (the Servian, the Aurelian); muralis of a wall (mural 1850s), inter-murale between the walls, im-murare to wall in (immure), French muraille and mur, Italian and Spanish muro, and German Mauer was borrowed from murus (the Berliner Mauer!); munire to fortify gave munitions, ammunition, and muniments — the documents that defend a title."],
  cinct: ["cingere(束、围)来自印欧语 *kenk-(束),同根有梵语 kāñcī(腰带)、立陶宛语 kinkyti(套马)。罗马人 cingere 束腰、围城;cinctus 束腰(罗马人分\"束腰\"与\"松袍\"),cingulum 腰带(cingulum militiae 军人的腰带——被剥去等于革职),prae-cingere 先束(precinct 是\"围起的区\"——教堂区、选区),suc-cinctus 束在下面——利落(succinct 1530 年代\"扎紧的\"→简洁),cincture 腰带,shingles 是 cingulus 带状疱疹,enceinte 围墙(法语,也指\"怀孕\"——in-cincta 不束带的),cinch 是西班牙语 cincha 马肚带(\"稳操\"),Cinturón。",
    "cingere (to gird, surround) comes from PIE *kenk- (to gird), shared with Sanskrit kāñcī (a girdle), Lithuanian kinkyti (to harness). Romans cingere their waist and a besieged town; cinctus girt (Romans were girt or ungirt — ready or at ease), cingulum a belt (the cingulum militiae, the soldier's belt — stripped from the cashiered), prae-cingere to gird in front (a precinct is the enclosed ground — of a church, a ward), suc-cinctus girt up from below — trim (succinct 1530s, tucked up → concise), cincture, shingles cingulus, the girdling rash, enceinte a wall (French; also pregnant — in-cincta, ungirt), cinch Spanish cincha, a saddle-girth (a sure thing)."],
  sculp: ["sculpere(刻)是 scalpere(刮、刻)的变体,印欧语 *(s)kel-(切),同根有 scalpellum(小刀——scalpel)、希腊语 skallein(锄)、英语 shell、shield、德语 Schale。罗马人 sculpere 刻石、刻宝石;sculptura 雕刻(sculpture 1400 年代),sculptor 雕刻家(1630 年代),scalpel 手术刀(1740 年代),scalp 头皮无关(北欧语),sculptural,scultore 意大利语(米开朗基罗自称 scultore),escultor。",
    "sculpere (to carve) is a variant of scalpere (to scratch, engrave), PIE *(s)kel- (to cut), shared with scalpellum (a small knife — scalpel), Greek skallein (to hoe), English shell and shield, German Schale. Romans sculpere stone and gems; sculptura carving (sculpture 1400s), sculptor (1630s), scalpel (1740s), sculptural, Italian scultore (Michelangelo signed himself so), Spanish escultor; scalp is unrelated (Norse)."],
  ov: ["ovum(卵)来自印欧语 *h₂ōwyom(卵,由 *h₂ewi- 鸟派生),同根有希腊语 ōion、英语 egg(北欧语 egg)、古英语 ǣg、德语 Ei、俄语 jajco、avis(鸟)。罗马人 ovum 是蛋(宴席 ab ovo usque ad mala\"从蛋到苹果\"——从头到尾;贺拉斯用 ab ovo 指从丽达之卵讲起);ovalis 蛋形(oval 1570 年代),ovarium 卵巢(1650 年代),ovulum 小卵——排卵(ovulation 1830 年代),oviparous 卵生,ovoid,œuf 法语,uovo 意大利语,huevo 西班牙语,ovo 葡萄牙语,ovation 与此无关(ovare 欢呼)。",
    "ovum (an egg) comes from PIE *h₂ōwyom (egg, from *h₂ewi- bird), shared with Greek ōion, English egg (from Norse), Old English ǣg, German Ei, Russian jajco, avis (a bird). The Roman dinner ran ab ovo usque ad mala, from egg to apples — start to finish; Horace's ab ovo begins a tale from Leda's egg; ovalis egg-shaped (oval 1570s), ovarium (1650s), ovulum a little egg — ovulation (1830s), oviparous, ovoid, French œuf, Italian uovo, Spanish huevo; ovation is unrelated (ovare, to cheer)."],
  pud: ["pudere(使羞愧)词源不明,可能来自 *(s)peud-(推开)。罗马人 pudor 是羞耻心——也是荣誉感(pudor 是罗马道德的核心之一),pudicus 贞洁的(pudicitia 是已婚妇女之德,有专门的女神),pudendum 该羞的部位(1630 年代医学),im-pudens 无耻(impudent 1380 年代),re-pudiare 推开——休妻(repudiate;repudium 是罗马法的离婚声明),pudibund 羞怯的,pudeur 法语羞耻,pudor 西班牙语。",
    "pudere (to make ashamed) is of uncertain origin, perhaps *(s)peud- (to push away). Roman pudor was shame — and the sense of honour that fears it (a pillar of Roman morals), pudicus chaste (pudicitia the married woman's virtue, with its own goddess), pudendum the part to be ashamed of (medical 1630s), im-pudens shameless (impudent 1380s), re-pudiare to push away — to divorce a wife (repudiate; repudium was the Roman law's notice of divorce), pudibund bashful, French pudeur, Spanish pudor."],
  cop: ["copia(丰富)是 co-ops——ops(资源、财富)加 co-:\"资源齐备\"。罗马人的 copia 是充足、也是军队(copiae 部队——人力资源!);copiosus 丰富的(copious 1380 年代),cornu-copiae 丰饶之角(哺育宙斯的羊角),copia 在中世纪转义为\"誊本\"——手稿的\"丰富\"就是多抄几份(copy 1300 年代;copyright 1735,copier),copier 法语抄,copioso,copia 西班牙语副本,in-ops 贫乏(inopia),op-ulentus 富有(opulent),Ops 是丰饶女神(萨图尔努斯之妻),office 是 opi-ficium 做工。",
    "copia (plenty) is co-ops — ops (resources, wealth) with co-: resources at hand. Roman copia was abundance and also troops (copiae — the manpower!); copiosus plentiful (copious 1380s), cornu-copiae the horn of plenty (the goat's horn that fed Zeus), copia in the Middle Ages came to mean a transcript — the plenty of a manuscript is its extra copies (copy 1300s; copyright 1735, copier), French copier, Italian copioso, Spanish copia, in-ops poor (inopia), op-ulentus wealthy (opulent), Ops the goddess of plenty, Saturn's wife, office opi-ficium, the doing of work."],
  nex: ["nectere(绑、连接,分词 nexus)来自印欧语 *ned-(绑),同根有英语 net(网)、nettle(荨麻——用作纤维)、德语 Netz、梵语 nahyati(绑)、拉丁语 nodus(结——node、noose)。罗马人 nectere 编织花环;nexus 是罗马法的债务奴役——债务人把自己\"绑\"给债权人(前 326 年废除),nexus 联系(1660 年代),an-nectere 连上(annex 1300 年代——附加、吞并),con-nectere 连在一起(connect、connection),nexum 债契,adnexa 附件(医学),denouement 是 dis-nodare 解结(法语 dénouement)——剧情的\"解开\"。",
    "nectere (to bind, participle nexus) comes from PIE *ned- (to bind), shared with English net and nettle (the fibre plant), German Netz, Sanskrit nahyati (binds), Latin nodus (a knot — node, noose). Romans nectere garlands; nexus was Roman law's debt-bondage — the debtor bound himself to his creditor (abolished 326 BC), nexus a link (1660s), an-nectere to tie on (annex 1300s — to attach, to seize territory), con-nectere to bind together (connect, connection), nexum the debt-bond, adnexa the appendages in anatomy, denouement dis-nodare, the unknotting of a plot."],
  custod: ["custos(看守,属格 custodis)来自印欧语 *(s)keudʰ-(遮盖、藏)存疑,同根的可能有希腊语 keuthein(藏)、英语 hide、hut、德语 Hütte。罗马的 custos 是守卫、监护人;custodia 看守、囚禁(custody 1400 年代——监护也是拘留),custodian 1780 年代,Quis custodiet ipsos custodes?\"谁来看守看守者\"(尤维纳利斯),custodire 看守,custodian,Custos Rotulorum 是英国郡的档案保管官,custode 意大利语看门人,custodio 西班牙语。",
    "custos (a guard, genitive custodis) may come from PIE *(s)keudʰ- (to cover, hide), with Greek keuthein (to hide), English hide and hut, German Hütte as possible cousins. The Roman custos was a watchman or guardian; custodia guarding and confinement (custody 1400s — guardianship and detention alike), custodian 1780s, Quis custodiet ipsos custodes? who will guard the guards (Juvenal), custodire to keep, Custos Rotulorum the English county's keeper of the rolls, Italian custode a caretaker, Spanish custodio."],
  lib: ["libra(秤、磅)可能与希腊语 litra(重量单位)同借自地中海某种前印欧语。罗马人的 libra 是天平和一磅(约 327 克)——货币 as 是一磅铜;libra 的缩写 lb 至今是磅,£ 是 libra 的花体,意大利 lira、土耳其 lira 都是 libra;Libra 天平座,librare 称量、平衡——equi-librium 平衡(1600 年代),de-liberare 仔细称量——deliberate(斟酌),librarius 与此无关(liber 书),level 是 libella 小天平(经法语 livel),livre 法语磅/古币,libra 西班牙语磅。",
    "libra (a balance, a pound) was probably borrowed, like Greek litra (a weight), from a pre-Indo-European Mediterranean tongue. Roman libra was the scales and a pound (about 327 g) — the as coin was a pound of bronze; lb is still the pound's abbreviation and £ a flourished libra; the Italian lira and the Turkish lira are libra; Libra the constellation, librare to weigh, to balance — equi-librium (1600s), de-liberare to weigh carefully — deliberate, level libella, a little balance, through French livel, French livre a pound and an old coin, Spanish libra."],
  libr: ["liber(书,属格 libri)本义是树皮内层——罗马人最早在树皮上写字;词源与 liber(自由)无关,来自 *leub-(剥)——英语 leaf、德语 Laub(叶)同源。libellus 小书——libel(诽谤:先指原告的小书状,1300 年代),librarius 抄书人,libraria 书铺——library 1370 年代(法语 librairie 至今是书店!),libretto 意大利语\"小书\"——歌剧脚本(1740 年代),livre 法语书,libro 意大利语/西班牙语,ex libris 藏书票\"来自…的书\",librettist。",
    "liber (a book, genitive libri) was first the inner bark of a tree — the earliest Roman writing surface; unrelated to liber (free), it comes from *leub- (to peel), kin to English leaf and German Laub (foliage). libellus a little book — libel (first the plaintiff's written statement, 1300s), librarius a copyist, libraria a bookshop — library 1370s (French librairie is still a bookshop!), libretto Italian \"little book\" — an opera's text (1740s), French livre, Italian and Spanish libro, ex libris from the books of, librettist."],
  iter: ["iterum(再)来自印欧语 *i-tero-(另一个)——与 is(那)同根,与 ire(走)无关。iterare 重复(iterate 1530 年代;iteration 计算机的循环 1960 年代),re-iterare 一再重复(reiterate 1520 年代),iterative 反复的;iter(路)是另一个词:itinerarium 路程记(itinerary),itinerant 巡回的,journey 无关。",
    "iterum (again) comes from PIE *i-tero- (the other one) — kin to is (that), not to ire (to go). iterare to repeat (iterate 1530s; the computer's iteration 1960s), re-iterare to say over and over (reiterate 1520s), iterative; iter (a journey) is a separate word: itinerarium an account of a journey (itinerary), itinerant travelling."],
  frat: ["frater(兄弟)是印欧语 *bʰréh₂tēr:希腊语 phratēr(氏族成员)、梵语 bhrātṛ、英语 brother、德语 Bruder、俄语 brat。罗马人 frater 是兄弟、也是同僚(fratres Arvales 阿尔瓦兄弟祭司团);fraternus 兄弟的(fraternal),fraternitas 兄弟情——中世纪的行会、修会(fraternity 1300 年代;美国大学的 frat 1890 年代),fratricida 杀兄弟者(fratricide;罗慕路斯杀雷穆斯),frère 法语,fra 意大利语修士(Fra Angelico),friar 是 frère 经英语——修士,confrère 同行,Liberté, égalité, fraternité 1790 年代;西班牙语 hermano 是另一个词(germanus)。",
    "frater (brother) is Indo-European *bʰréh₂tēr: Greek phratēr (a clansman), Sanskrit bhrātṛ, English brother, German Bruder, Russian brat. Roman frater was a brother and a colleague (the fratres Arvales priesthood); fraternus brotherly (fraternal), fraternitas brotherhood — the medieval guild and order (fraternity 1300s; the American college frat 1890s), fratricida a brother-slayer (fratricide; Romulus and Remus), French frère, Italian fra a friar (Fra Angelico), friar frère in English mouths, confrère, Liberté, égalité, fraternité 1790s; Spanish hermano is a different word (germanus)."],
  herman: ["germanus(同父母的、真正的)来自 germen(芽、种)——\"同一种子的\";印欧语 *ǵenh₁-(生)。罗马人说 frater germanus 是同父母的亲兄弟(区别于堂表);晚期伊比利亚拉丁语直接把 germanus 当\"兄弟\"——西班牙语 hermano、葡萄牙语 irmão、加泰罗尼亚语 germà;germanus 也指\"真正的\"——germane 相关的(1600 年代,哈姆雷特),germ 是 germen 芽——细菌(1880 年代巴斯德时代),germinate 发芽,germination;Germania 日耳曼尼亚是另一个词(可能是凯尔特语)。",
    "germanus (of the same parents, genuine) comes from germen (a sprout, a seed) — of one seed; PIE *ǵenh₁- (to beget). Roman frater germanus was a full brother, as against cousins; late Iberian Latin let germanus stand alone for brother — Spanish hermano, Portuguese irmão, Catalan germà; germanus also meant real — germane, relevant (1600s, Hamlet), germ germen, a sprout — a microbe (1880s, Pasteur's age), germinate, germination; Germania is a different word, probably Celtic."],
  dext: ["dexter(右、灵巧)来自印欧语 *deks-(右、南——面朝日出时右手指南),同根有希腊语 dexios、梵语 dakṣiṇa(右、南——德干高原 Deccan 是\"南方\"!)、哥特语 taihswa、爱尔兰语 dess。罗马占卜以右为吉(dexter)、左为凶(sinister——sinister 至今是\"阴险\");dexteritas 灵巧(dexterity 1520 年代),ambi-dexter 双手灵巧,dextrose 右旋糖(使偏振光右旋),dextro-,destro 意大利语右,diestro 西班牙语灵巧的(斗牛士),droit 法语右是另一个词(directus)。",
    "dexter (right, skilful) comes from PIE *deks- (right, south — the right hand points south when one faces the sunrise), shared with Greek dexios, Sanskrit dakṣiṇa (right, south — the Deccan is the South!), Gothic taihswa, Irish dess. Roman augury took the right (dexter) as lucky and the left (sinister) as ill-omened — sinister still is; dexteritas skill (dexterity 1520s), ambi-dexter, dextrose the sugar that turns polarised light rightward, Italian destro right, Spanish diestro skilful (the bullfighter); French droit is another word (directus)."],
  rog: ["rogare(问、请求、提案)来自印欧语 *h₃reǵ-(伸直)——伸手去求;与 regere 同根。罗马官员 rogare 向公民大会提出法案(rogatio 法案,leges rogatae),rogare 也是求神、求人;inter-rogare 询问(interrogate),ab-rogare 废除法案(abrogate),de-rogare 部分废除——贬损(derogatory),ar-rogare 归于自己——傲慢(arrogant\"为自己索取\"),pro-rogare 延期(prorogue 议会休会),sur-rogare 替补选出(surrogate 代理),pre-rogativa 是首先投票的百人队(prerogative 特权),rogation days 祈祷日,rogar 西班牙语请求,derogate。",
    "rogare (to ask, to propose a law) comes from PIE *h₃reǵ- (to stretch straight) — to reach out and ask; kin to regere. A Roman magistrate rogare a bill before the assembly (rogatio; leges rogatae), and rogare was also to entreat gods and men; inter-rogare to question (interrogate), ab-rogare to repeal (abrogate), de-rogare to repeal in part — to detract (derogatory), ar-rogare to claim for oneself (arrogant), pro-rogare to extend a term (prorogue a parliament), sur-rogare to elect a substitute (surrogate), prae-rogativa the century that voted first (prerogative), Rogation Days, Spanish rogar to beg."],
  scind: ["scindere(撕、劈,分词 scissus)来自印欧语 *skei-d-(劈),同根有希腊语 skhizein(schism、schizo-)、梵语 chinatti(切)、英语 shed(分开)、德语 scheiden、哥特语 skaidan、拉丁语 scire?(存疑)。罗马人 scindere 撕衣(哀悼)、劈木;scissio 分裂,re-scindere 撕回——撤销(rescind 1630 年代),ab-scindere 切掉(abscissa 横坐标是\"被切下的线段\",1690 年代),scissors 是 cisorium(caedere)受 scissus 影响的拼写,scission 分裂,exscind 剪除,scindere 意大利语,escindir 西班牙语分离。",
    "scindere (to tear, split, participle scissus) comes from PIE *skei-d- (to split), shared with Greek skhizein (schism, schizo-), Sanskrit chinatti (cuts), English shed (to part), German scheiden, Gothic skaidan. Romans scindere garments in grief and wood for fire; scissio a splitting, re-scindere to tear back — to annul (rescind 1630s), ab-scindere to cut off (the abscissa is the cut-off segment, 1690s), scissors cisorium (caedere) respelt under scissus, scission, exscind, Italian scindere, Spanish escindir."],
  juv: ["iuvare(帮助、使愉快)来自印欧语 *h₁yeu-(帮)存疑,同根的可能有梵语 avati(帮)、iuvenis?否(另一个词)。罗马人 iuvare 帮助,也说 iuvat\"令人高兴\"(维吉尔 forsan et haec olim meminisse iuvabit\"也许有一天连这些也会成为愉快的回忆\");ad-iuvare 帮助(adjutant 副官——\"帮手\",1600 年代;aide 法语,aid 英语 1400 年代,ayudar 西班牙语,aiutare 意大利语),iucundus 令人愉快的(jocund),coadjutor 助理主教,adjuvant 佐剂(疫苗),aide-de-camp 侍从副官。",
    "iuvare (to help, to please) may come from PIE *h₁yeu- (to help), with Sanskrit avati (helps) a possible cousin — iuvenis (young) is another word. Romans iuvare helped, and said iuvat, it pleases (Virgil's forsan et haec olim meminisse iuvabit, perhaps one day even this will be a joy to remember); ad-iuvare to assist (adjutant the helper-officer, 1600s; French aide, English aid 1400s, Spanish ayudar, Italian aiutare), iucundus pleasant (jocund), coadjutor an assisting bishop, adjuvant the vaccine's helper, aide-de-camp."],
  juven: ["iuvenis(年轻的)来自印欧语 *h₂yu-h₁en-(年轻),同根有梵语 yuvan、英语 young、德语 jung、立陶宛语 jaunas、爱尔兰语 óg;根 *h₂eyu-(生命力)也给了 aevum(时代)、aeternus。罗马的 iuvenis 是 20–45 岁的壮年(iuventus 青年团、Iuventas 女神);iuvenilis 年轻的(juvenile 1620 年代),iunior 较年轻(junior),re-iuvenescere 返老还童(rejuvenate 1800 年代),jeune 法语年轻,giovane 意大利语,joven 西班牙语,juniper 无关,Juventus 都灵的\"青年\"足球队(1897),June 是 Iuno(存疑)。",
    "iuvenis (young) comes from PIE *h₂yu-h₁en- (young), shared with Sanskrit yuvan, English young, German jung, Lithuanian jaunas, Irish óg; the root *h₂eyu- (vital force) also gave aevum (an age) and aeternus. A Roman iuvenis was a man in his prime, 20 to 45 (the iuventus, the goddess Iuventas); iuvenilis youthful (juvenile 1620s), iunior younger (junior), re-iuvenescere to grow young again (rejuvenate 1800s), French jeune, Italian giovane, Spanish joven, Juventus Turin's Youth football club (1897); June is Juno's month (disputed)."],
  al: ["alere(养育)来自印欧语 *h₂el-(养、长),同根有英语 old(长成的)、德语 alt、哥特语 alan(长大)、希腊语 al-dainein(使长)、爱尔兰语 alim(养)。罗马人 alere 养孩子、养牲畜;alimentum 食物(aliment;alimony 1650 年代——供养),alumnus 被养的——学生(alumnus 1640 年代,alumna),altus 长高了的——高、深(altitude、exalt),adultus 长成的(adult),ad-olescere 正在长(adolescent),co-alescere 长在一起(coalesce、coalition),ob-solescere 长过时(obsolete),pro-les 后代(proletarius 只有孩子的人——proletariat),Alma Mater\"养育的母亲\",alible 有营养的。",
    "alere (to nourish) comes from PIE *h₂el- (to nourish, grow), shared with English old (grown up), German alt, Gothic alan (to grow), Greek al-dainein (to make grow), Irish alim (I rear). Romans alere children and herds; alimentum food (aliment; alimony 1650s — maintenance), alumnus one nourished — a pupil (alumnus 1640s, alumna), altus grown tall — high, deep (altitude, exalt), adultus grown up (adult), ad-olescere growing up (adolescent), co-alescere to grow together (coalesce, coalition), ob-solescere to grow out of use (obsolete), pro-les offspring (proletarius, a man with only children to give the state — proletariat), Alma Mater the nourishing mother."],
  hon: ["honos/honor(荣誉、官职)词源不明——可能与 onus(负担)有关(荣誉是负担)。罗马的 honores 是公职(cursus honorum 官职阶梯:财务官→市政官→裁判官→执政官),Honos 是有神庙的神;honestus 光荣的——honest 先是\"体面的\"(1300 年代),17 世纪才是\"诚实\",honorarium 是给自由职业者的\"荣誉性酬金\"(不算工资),honorary 荣誉的,honorificus,dishonour,honneur 法语(Légion d'honneur 1802),onore 意大利语,honra 西班牙语(honra 是名誉、honor 是荣誉——两形并存)。",
    "honos/honor (honour, public office) is of unknown origin — perhaps linked to onus (a burden). The Roman honores were the magistracies (the cursus honorum: quaestor, aedile, praetor, consul), and Honos a god with temples; honestus honourable — honest meant respectable in the 1300s and truthful only in the 17th century, honorarium the professional's \"honour-fee\" (not a wage), honorary, honorificus, dishonour, French honneur (the Légion d'honneur 1802), Italian onore, Spanish honra and honor side by side."],
  man3: ["manere(停留,分词 mansus)来自印欧语 *men-(留)——与 mens(心)是不同的根,同根有希腊语 menein(留)、波斯语 mān(家)、亚美尼亚语 mnam。罗马人 manere 留下、过夜;mansio 驿站(mansion 1300 年代——先是住所,后指大宅;法语 maison 就是家!),re-manere 留下(remain、remnant),per-manere 一直留(permanent),im-manens 内在的(immanent 经院哲学),manor 是 manoir 住处——庄园,ménage 家庭(mansionaticum),menagerie 动物园(家养),manse 牧师住宅,mainstay 无关,mansard 无关(建筑师姓),Mansión。",
    "manere (to stay, participle mansus) comes from PIE *men- (to remain) — a different root from mens (mind); cognates are Greek menein (to stay), Persian mān (a house), Armenian mnam. Romans manere stayed, stayed the night; mansio a halting-place (mansion 1300s — a dwelling before a great house; French maison is simply home!), re-manere to stay behind (remain, remnant), per-manere to stay throughout (permanent), im-manens dwelling within (immanent, in the schoolmen), manor manoir, a dwelling — an estate, ménage a household (mansionaticum), menagerie the housing of animals, manse the minister's house; mansard is unrelated (an architect's name)."],
  equ2: ["equus(马)是印欧语 *h₁éḱwos:希腊语 hippos、梵语 aśva、古英语 eoh、哥特语 aiƕa、高卢语 epos(Epona 马神)。罗马的 equites 骑士阶层(能自备马的公民,后成第二等级);equester 骑士的(equestrian 1650 年代),equinus 马的(equine),Equus 属名,equitatus 骑兵,equiferus 野马,eques 骑士;caballus(驽马)是口语,给了罗曼语——cheval、cavallo、caballo;horse 是日耳曼词,与此无关;hippo- 是希腊线(hippopotamus 河马、hippodrome、Philip 爱马者)。",
    "equus (a horse) is Indo-European *h₁éḱwos: Greek hippos, Sanskrit aśva, Old English eoh, Gothic aiƕa, Gaulish epos (the horse-goddess Epona). Rome's equites were the knights, citizens who could furnish a horse, later the second order; equester of knights (equestrian 1650s), equinus of horses (equine), Equus the genus, equitatus cavalry, equiferus a wild horse; caballus (a nag) was the everyday word and became the Romance horse — cheval, cavallo, caballo; horse is Germanic and unrelated; hippo- is the Greek line (hippopotamus the river-horse, hippodrome, Philip the horse-lover)."],
  caball: ["caballus(驽马、干活的马)是拉丁语借词——可能来自高卢语或巴尔干语(希腊语 kaballēs 也是借的);它是 equus 的口语替身,所以罗曼语的\"马\"都是它:法语 cheval、意大利语 cavallo、西班牙语 caballo、葡萄牙语 cavalo。caballarius 骑马人——中世纪的骑士:chevalier(法语)、cavaliere(意大利语)、caballero(西班牙语——后指\"绅士\"),chivalry 是 chevalerie 骑士精神(1300 年代),cavalry 骑兵(1540 年代经意大利语 cavalleria),cavalier 骑士——英国内战的保王党(1640 年代),cavalcade 骑行队,chevalet 画架(\"小马\"),cheval-de-frise 拒马,cavallo 意大利语,cabalgar 西班牙语骑。",
    "caballus (a nag, a work-horse) is a loanword in Latin — Gaulish or Balkan (Greek kaballēs was borrowed too); it was the everyday stand-in for equus, so every Romance horse is caballus: French cheval, Italian cavallo, Spanish caballo, Portuguese cavalo. caballarius a horseman — the medieval knight: chevalier, cavaliere, caballero (later simply a gentleman), chivalry chevalerie (1300s), cavalry (1540s via Italian cavalleria), cavalier a horseman — the royalists of the English Civil War (1640s), cavalcade, chevalet an easel (a little horse), cheval-de-frise the spiked barrier, Spanish cabalgar to ride."],
  per: ["periculum(试验、危险)来自 *per-(尝试、冒险),与 experiri(尝试)、peritus(有经验的)同根;印欧语 *per-(尝试)也给了希腊语 peira(尝试——pirate 是\"试运气的人\"!、empirical 经验的)、英语 fear(古义\"危险\")、德语 Gefahr(危险)、fahren?否。罗马人 periculum 是审判、也是危险;periculosus 危险的(perilous),peril 经法语 péril(1200 年代),ex-perientia 试出来的知识(experience),ex-perimentum 试验(experiment),ex-pertus 试过的(expert),peritus 内行(peritia),im-peritus 外行,pericolo 意大利语,peligro 西班牙语(音位换位!),danger 是另一个词(dominium)。",
    "periculum (a trial, a danger) comes from *per- (to try, to risk), kin to experiri (to try) and peritus (experienced); PIE *per- (to try) also gave Greek peira (an attempt — a pirate is one who tries his luck!, empirical), English fear (once danger), German Gefahr (danger). Roman periculum was a trial at law and a risk; periculosus dangerous (perilous), peril through French péril (1200s), ex-perientia knowledge won by trial (experience), ex-perimentum a test (experiment), ex-pertus tried (expert), peritus skilled (peritia), im-peritus unskilled, Italian pericolo, Spanish peligro (with metathesis!); danger is a different word (dominium)."],
  vigil: ["vigil(醒着的)与 vigere(有活力)来自印欧语 *weǵ-(强壮、醒),同根有英语 wake、watch、German wachen、wecken(唤醒)、梵语 vāja(力)、拉丁语 vegetus(活泼的——vegetable 是\"有生长力的\"!)。罗马的 vigiles 是奥古斯都前 6 年设的消防夜警队;vigilia 守夜——军营的四班夜哨、教会的节前守夜(vigil 1200 年代),vigilare 醒着(vigilant 1480 年代),vigilantia,vigilante 是西班牙语\"警惕者\"经美国西部(1860 年代),sur-veiller 从上看守(surveillance 法国大革命 1790 年代),réveiller 唤醒(reveille 起床号),vegetare 使有活力,vigour/vigor 是 vigere。",
    "vigil (awake) and vigere (to be vigorous) come from PIE *weǵ- (to be strong, awake), shared with English wake and watch, German wachen, wecken (to rouse), Sanskrit vāja (strength), Latin vegetus (lively — a vegetable is what has growing-force!). Rome's vigiles were the night watch and fire brigade Augustus founded in 6 BC; vigilia a night-watch — the camp's four watches, the Church's eve of a feast (vigil 1200s), vigilare to stay awake (vigilant 1480s), vigilantia, vigilante Spanish for a watchful man, through the American West (1860s), sur-veiller to watch over (surveillance, the Revolution's 1790s), réveiller to wake (reveille), vigour from vigere."],
  pict: ["pingere(画,分词 pictus)来自印欧语 *peik-(刻、装饰、涂色),同根有希腊语 poikilos(杂色)、梵语 piṃśati(装饰)、英语 file(锉)、德语 Feile、古教会斯拉夫语 pisati(写——俄语 pisat'!)。罗马人 pingere 画画、也刺青(Picti 皮克特人是\"涂色的人\");pictura 画(picture 1420 年代),pictor 画家,pictorius,de-pingere 描绘(depict),pigmentum 颜料(pigment;pimento 甜椒是它经西班牙语——先指香料!),picturesque 1700 年代经意大利语 pittoresco,pittore 意大利语画家,pintar 西班牙语画,peindre 法语,pinto 花斑马,paint 是 peint 经法语(1200 年代)。",
    "pingere (to paint, participle pictus) comes from PIE *peik- (to cut, adorn, colour), shared with Greek poikilos (many-coloured), Sanskrit piṃśati (adorns), English file (the tool), German Feile, Old Church Slavonic pisati (to write — Russian pisat'!). Romans pingere pictures and tattoos (the Picti are the painted people); pictura a painting (picture 1420s), pictor a painter, de-pingere to portray (depict), pigmentum colouring matter (pigment; pimento is it through Spanish — first a spice!), picturesque 1700s via Italian pittoresco, Italian pittore, Spanish pintar, French peindre, pinto the piebald horse, paint peint through French (1200s)."],
  natat: ["natare(游泳)是 nare(游)的频繁式,印欧语 *sneh₂-(游、流),同根有希腊语 nein/nēkhein(游)、梵语 snāti(洗浴)、爱尔兰语 snáim(游)、拉丁语 Neptunus?(存疑)。罗马人 natare 在台伯河游泳(男孩教育的一部分);natatio 游泳(natation),natatorium 游泳池(1890 年代美国),nager 法语游泳,nuotare 意大利语,nadar 西班牙语,natant 浮游的,super-natant 上清液,Nereid?否;swim 是日耳曼词。",
    "natare (to swim) is the frequentative of nare, PIE *sneh₂- (to swim, flow), shared with Greek nein/nēkhein (to swim), Sanskrit snāti (bathes), Irish snáim (I swim). Roman boys learned to natare in the Tiber; natatio swimming (natation), natatorium a swimming pool (American 1890s), French nager, Italian nuotare, Spanish nadar, natant floating, super-natant the liquid above a sediment; swim is Germanic."],
  pecu: ["pecu(牲畜)来自印欧语 *peḱu-(牲畜——财产),同根有梵语 paśu(牲畜)、英语 fee(古英语 feoh 牲畜、财产!)、德语 Vieh(牲畜)、哥特语 faihu(财产)。罗马最早的财富是牛羊,所以 pecunia 是钱(pecuniary 1500 年代),peculium 是奴隶或儿子自己的\"小牲群\"——私产(peculiar 先指\"自己专有的\",1600 年代才转\"奇特\"),peculatus 挪用公款(\"牲畜\"→\"公款\";peculate、peculation),pecus 牲畜(拉美的 pecuarista 牧场主),impecunious 没钱的,fee 是它的日耳曼亲戚,fief/feudal 也是(faihu→feodum)。",
    "pecu (cattle) comes from PIE *peḱu- (livestock — wealth), shared with Sanskrit paśu (cattle), English fee (Old English feoh, cattle, property!), German Vieh (cattle), Gothic faihu (property). Rome's first wealth was in herds, so pecunia is money (pecuniary 1500s), peculium a slave's or son's own little herd — private property (peculiar meant one's own until the 1600s made it odd), peculatus embezzlement of public money (peculate, peculation), pecus livestock (the Latin American pecuarista, a rancher), impecunious penniless; fee is its Germanic cousin, and so are fief and feudal (faihu → feodum)."],
  avi: ["avis(鸟)来自印欧语 *h₂ewi-(鸟),同根有希腊语 aietos(鹰)、oiōnos(大鸟、征兆)、梵语 vi(鸟)、亚美尼亚语 haw、威尔士语 hwyad(鸭)、拉丁语 ovum(卵)。罗马占卜看鸟(au-spicium 是 avi-spicium\"看鸟\"——auspices、auspicious;augur 可能也是\"鸟\"+ garrire 叫?存疑),aviarium 鸟舍(aviary),aviatio 是 1863 年法国人 La Landelle 造的\"飞行\"(aviation、aviator),avicula 小鸟,ave 西班牙语鸟,oiseau 法语是 aucellus 小鸟,uccello 意大利语,ostrich 是 avis struthio 鸵鸟,bustard 是 avis tarda 慢鸟,pájaro 是 passer 麻雀。",
    "avis (a bird) comes from PIE *h₂ewi- (bird), shared with Greek aietos (an eagle), oiōnos (a great bird, an omen), Sanskrit vi (a bird), Armenian haw, Welsh hwyad (a duck), Latin ovum (an egg). Roman augury watched birds (au-spicium is avi-spicium, bird-watching — auspices, auspicious), aviarium a bird-house (aviary), aviatio the Frenchman La Landelle's 1863 coinage for flying (aviation, aviator), avicula a little bird, Spanish ave, French oiseau aucellus, a little bird, Italian uccello, ostrich avis struthio, bustard avis tarda, the slow bird, Spanish pájaro from passer, a sparrow."],
  miser: ["miser(可怜、悲惨)词源不明——可能与希腊语 misos(恨)有关(存疑)。罗马人 miser 是不幸的人;miseria 苦难(misery 1370 年代),misereri 怜悯——Miserere 是《诗篇》51 篇的开头\"求你怜悯我\"(阿莱格里的名曲),miseri-cordia 怜悯之心(misericord 是唱诗席下的\"怜悯小凳\"、也是刺死伤者的\"慈悲匕首\"),miserabilis 可怜的(miserable;Les Misérables 1862),miser 在英语 1560 年代转为\"守财奴\"(自己可怜自己),commiserate 同情(1590 年代),misero 意大利语,mísero 西班牙语。",
    "miser (wretched) is of unknown origin — a link to Greek misos (hatred) is doubtful. Roman miser was the unfortunate man; miseria hardship (misery 1370s), misereri to pity — the Miserere is Psalm 51's opening, have mercy on me (Allegri's setting), miseri-cordia a pitying heart (the misericord is the choir-stall's mercy-ledge, and the dagger that ended a wounded man's pain), miserabilis pitiable (miserable; Les Misérables 1862), miser turned in English by the 1560s into the hoarder who makes himself wretched, commiserate (1590s), Italian misero, Spanish mísero."],
  cup: ["cupere(渴望)来自印欧语 *kup-(冒烟、沸腾、渴望),同根有梵语 kupyati(激动、发怒)、古教会斯拉夫语 kypĕti(沸腾)、英语 无。罗马人 cupere 想要;cupido 欲望——Cupido 是欲望之神(维纳斯之子,希腊的 Eros;Cupid),cupiditas 贪欲(cupidity 1440 年代),con-cupiscentia 是奥古斯丁的\"情欲\"(concupiscence),cupidus 贪求的,covet 是 cupiditare 经法语 coveitier(1200 年代;摩西十诫\"不可贪恋\"),covetous,codicia 西班牙语贪婪,cupido 意大利语丘比特。",
    "cupere (to desire) comes from PIE *kup- (to smoke, boil, crave), shared with Sanskrit kupyati (is agitated, angry), Old Church Slavonic kypĕti (to boil). Romans cupere wanted; cupido desire — Cupido the god of desire, Venus' son, Greek Eros (Cupid), cupiditas greed (cupidity 1440s), con-cupiscentia Augustine's lust of the flesh (concupiscence), cupidus eager, covet cupiditare through French coveitier (1200s; the commandment's thou shalt not covet), covetous, Spanish codicia greed, Italian cupido."],
  estim: ["aestimare(估价)来自 aes(铜、钱)+ *tem-(切)?存疑——古义\"定铜价\"。罗马法官 aestimare litem 评估赔偿;aestimatio 估价,ex-istimare 判断、认为(esteem 1400 年代经法语 estimer——先是\"估价\",后是\"看重\"),estimate 1530 年代,estimation,aim 是 aestimare 经法语 esmer——\"估算\"→\"瞄准\"(1300 年代)!,in-aestimabilis 无法估量(inestimable),estima 西班牙语敬重,stima 意大利语,self-esteem 1650 年代。",
    "aestimare (to value) may be aes (bronze, money) with *tem- (to cut) — to set a bronze price. A Roman judge aestimare litem, assessed the damages; aestimatio a valuation, ex-istimare to judge, to think (esteem 1400s through French estimer — to price, then to prize), estimate 1530s, estimation, and aim is aestimare through French esmer — to reckon, then to take aim (1300s)!, in-aestimabilis beyond valuing (inestimable), Spanish estima regard, Italian stima, self-esteem 1650s."],
  morb: ["morbus(病)来自印欧语 *mer-(死、磨损)——与 mori(死)同根;或与 mordere(咬)有关。罗马人 morbus 是疾病,Morbus 有神庙?否——但 Febris(热病)有;morbidus 有病的(morbid 1650 年代先是医学的\"病态\",1830 年代转为\"病态心理\"),morbidity 发病率,morbilli 麻疹(\"小病\"),morbus Gallicus 法国病(梅毒,16 世纪),morboso 意大利语,mórbido 西班牙语柔软(意大利语转义!),morbific 致病的。",
    "morbus (disease) comes from PIE *mer- (to die, wear away) — kin to mori (to die); or from mordere (to bite). Roman morbus was sickness (Febris, fever, had temples of her own); morbidus diseased (morbid was medical in the 1650s and turned to the unwholesome mind by the 1830s), morbidity, morbilli the little disease — measles, morbus Gallicus the French disease of the 16th century (syphilis), Italian morboso, Spanish mórbido soft (an Italian twist!), morbific."],
  aed: ["aedes(神庙、房子)本义\"炉火处\",来自印欧语 *h₂eydʰ-(烧),同根有希腊语 aithein(烧)、aithēr(以太——上层的燃烧的天)、英语 oast(烘房)、爱尔兰语 áed(火)。罗马的 aedes 是单间神庙(与 templum 划出的圣区不同),aedilis 是管理神庙、公共建筑、市场、竞技会的市政官(aedile,前 494 年设),aedi-ficare 建房——edify 先指\"建造\",13 世纪转为\"造就(灵魂)\"(edification),aedificium 建筑(edifice 1380 年代),edile,edificio 意大利语/西班牙语。",
    "aedes (a temple, a house) was first the hearth-place, from PIE *h₂eydʰ- (to burn), shared with Greek aithein (to burn), aithēr (the upper burning air — ether), English oast (a drying-kiln), Irish áed (fire). The Roman aedes was a single-roomed temple (unlike the templum, an inaugurated precinct), and the aedilis the magistrate who kept temples, public buildings, markets and games (aedile, from 494 BC), aedi-ficare to build — edify meant to build until the 13th century turned it to building up the soul (edification), aedificium a building (edifice 1380s), Italian and Spanish edificio."],
  glor: ["gloria(荣耀)词源不明——可能来自 *gnō-ria(\"被知晓\"),与 gnoscere 有关(存疑)。罗马人的 gloria 是战功的名声(西塞罗写《论荣耀》);gloriosus 光荣的——也指\"吹牛的\"(普劳图斯《吹牛军人》Miles Gloriosus),Gloria 是弥撒的\"荣归主颂\"(Gloria in excelsis Deo),glorificare 使荣耀(glorify),gloriari 自夸(vainglory 空虚的荣耀),glory 1300 年代,gloire 法语,gloria 西班牙语/意大利语,Gloria 名字,glorious。",
    "gloria (glory) is of unknown origin — a link to gnoscere (to be known) is doubtful. Roman gloria was the renown of great deeds (Cicero wrote a De Gloria); gloriosus glorious and also boastful (Plautus' Miles Gloriosus, the braggart soldier), the Gloria the Mass's hymn (Gloria in excelsis Deo), glorificare to glorify, gloriari to boast (vainglory), glory 1300s, French gloire, Spanish and Italian gloria, Gloria the name, glorious."],
  puer: ["puer(男孩)来自印欧语 *peh₂u-(小、少),同根有 paucus(少)、pauper(穷)、parvus(小)、希腊语 pais(孩子——paed-)、梵语 putra(儿子)、英语 few。罗马的 puer 是男孩,也是奴隶(像法语 garçon);puerilis 孩子气的(puerile 1660 年代),puerperium 产褥期(puer-parere 生孩子),puella 女孩(puer 的阴性指小),pueritia 童年,Puer natus est\"婴孩为我们而生\"(圣诞入祭咏),puericulture 育儿。",
    "puer (a boy) comes from PIE *peh₂u- (small, few), kin to paucus (few), pauper (poor), parvus (small), Greek pais (a child — paed-), Sanskrit putra (a son), English few. The Roman puer was a boy and also a slave (like French garçon); puerilis boyish (puerile 1660s), puerperium childbed (puer-parere, to bear a child), puella a girl (the feminine diminutive), pueritia boyhood, Puer natus est the Christmas introit, unto us a child is born, puericulture child-rearing."],
  ferr: ["ferrum(铁)词源不明——可能与闪米特语 barzel(希伯来语)/parzillu(阿卡德语)同借自一种近东语言(铁器时代的技术词)。罗马的 ferrum 是铁、剑(ferro et igni 以铁与火);ferrarius 铁匠(Ferrari 是\"铁匠\"姓!),ferreus 铁的,ferratus 包铁的,fer 法语铁(chemin de fer 铁路),ferro 意大利语,hierro 西班牙语(f→h),ferrous/ferric 亚铁/正铁(1860 年代),ferrite,Fe 元素符号,farrier 蹄铁匠是 ferrarius 经法语,ferro-concrete 钢筋混凝土。",
    "ferrum (iron) is of unknown origin — probably borrowed, like Semitic barzel (Hebrew) and parzillu (Akkadian), from some Near Eastern tongue, a term of Iron Age technology. Roman ferrum was iron and the sword (ferro et igni, by iron and fire); ferrarius a smith (Ferrari is the smith's surname!), ferreus of iron, ferratus iron-shod, French fer (chemin de fer, the railway), Italian ferro, Spanish hierro (f → h), ferrous and ferric (1860s), ferrite, the symbol Fe, farrier ferrarius through French, ferro-concrete."],
  fle: ["flere(哭泣,分词 fletus)来自印欧语 *bʰleh₁-(嚎叫、吹),同根有英语 bleat(咩叫)、德语 blöken、拉脱维亚语 blêt。罗马人 flere 哭;fletus 泪水,flebilis 可悲的、哭哭啼啼的(feeble 是它经法语 feible!——\"该哭的\"→\"虚弱的\",1100 年代),foible 是同一个词的另一形(剑的弱段→小缺点),de-flere 哀悼,fievole 意大利语微弱,feblesse。",
    "flere (to weep, participle fletus) comes from PIE *bʰleh₁- (to howl, blow), shared with English bleat, German blöken, Latvian blêt. Romans flere wept; fletus tears, flebilis lamentable, tearful (feeble is it through French feible! — to be wept over → weak, 1100s), foible the same word in another French shape (the weak part of a blade → a small weakness), de-flere to mourn, Italian fievole faint."],
  lic: ["licere(被允许)来自印欧语 *leik-(留下?出价?)存疑;liceri 是\"出价\"(拍卖)。罗马人说 licet\"可以\";licentia 许可——也是放纵(licence 1360 年代;licentious 1530 年代),licitus 合法的(licit、illicit),leisure 是 licere 经法语 leisir\"被允许(的时间)\"(1300 年代)!,licentiate 持执照者(中世纪大学的 licentia docendi 教学许可),licenciado 西班牙语学士,scilicet(scire licet)\"即\",videlicet(viz.),lecito 意大利语。",
    "licere (to be allowed) is of uncertain origin; liceri is to bid at auction. Romans said licet, it is permitted; licentia permission — and licence taken (licence 1360s; licentious 1530s), licitus lawful (licit, illicit), leisure is licere through French leisir — time that is permitted (1300s)!, licentiate the holder of a licence (the medieval university's licentia docendi), Spanish licenciado a graduate, scilicet (scire licet) namely, videlicet (viz.), Italian lecito."],
  fiss: ["findere(劈,分词 fissus)来自印欧语 *bʰeid-(劈),同根有英语 bite(咬——劈开)、bit、boat(挖空的树干!)、德语 beißen、梵语 bhinatti(劈)。罗马人 findere 劈木;fissura 裂缝(fissure 1400 年代),fissilis 易裂的(fissile——核裂变 1930 年代),fissio 分裂(fission 1841 生物学、1939 核物理:迈特纳与弗里施借用),fissiparous 分裂繁殖,bifid 二裂,fender?否,hendidura 西班牙语裂缝(f→h)。",
    "findere (to split, participle fissus) comes from PIE *bʰeid- (to split), shared with English bite (to cleave), bit, boat (a hollowed trunk!), German beißen, Sanskrit bhinatti (splits). Romans findere logs; fissura a crack (fissure 1400s), fissilis splittable (fissile — of nuclei, 1930s), fissio a splitting (fission in biology 1841, in physics 1939 — Meitner and Frisch's borrowing), fissiparous, bifid, Spanish hendidura a cleft (f → h)."],
  od2: ["odi(我恨)是一个只有完成式的动词——\"我已恨了\"就是\"我恨\";来自印欧语 *h₃ed-(恨),同根有希腊语 odyssasthai(发怒——Odysseus\"被恨者\"!)、亚美尼亚语 ateam、古英语 atol(可怕)。odium 恨——odium theologicum 神学家之恨,odiosus 可恨的(odious 1380 年代),odio 意大利语/西班牙语恨,annoy 是 in odio\"在恨中\"经法语 enuier(1200 年代)!,ennui 是同一个词的法语形(1660 年代——无聊),noisome 是 annoy 的缩短+some,noyade 无关,odiar 西班牙语恨。",
    "odi (I hate) is a verb with only perfect forms — I have come to hate is I hate; PIE *h₃ed- (to hate), shared with Greek odyssasthai (to be angry — Odysseus, the hated one!), Armenian ateam, Old English atol (dreadful). odium hatred — the odium theologicum of divines, odiosus hateful (odious 1380s), Italian and Spanish odio, and annoy is in odio, in hatred, through French enuier (1200s)!, ennui the same word in French dress (1660s — boredom), noisome a clipped annoy with -some, Spanish odiar."],
  sepul: ["sepelire(埋葬,分词 sepultus)来自印欧语 *sep-(敬奉、处理)存疑,同根的可能有梵语 sapati(侍奉)、希腊语 hepein(照料)。罗马法禁止城内埋葬(十二表法),墓在城外道旁(Via Appia);sepulcrum 坟墓(sepulchre 1200 年代——圣墓教堂 Holy Sepulchre),sepultura 埋葬(sepulture),sepolcro 意大利语,sepulcro 西班牙语,sépulture 法语,sepulchral 阴森的(1610 年代),whited sepulchres 粉饰的坟墓(马太福音)。",
    "sepelire (to bury, participle sepultus) may come from PIE *sep- (to honour, handle), with Sanskrit sapati (serves) and Greek hepein (to tend) as possible cousins. Roman law forbade burial inside the city (the Twelve Tables), so tombs lined the roads outside (the Via Appia); sepulcrum a tomb (sepulchre 1200s — the Holy Sepulchre), sepultura burial (sepulture), Italian sepolcro, Spanish sepulcro, French sépulture, sepulchral (1610s), the whited sepulchres of Matthew."],
  lucr: ["lucrum(利润)来自印欧语 *leh₂u-(获得、享受),同根有希腊语 apo-lauein(享受)、英语 无(loot 无关),哥特语 laun(报酬)、德语 Lohn(工资)!罗马人 lucrum 是赢利(lucri causa 为了钱);lucrativus 有利可图(lucrative 1400 年代),lucre 钱财——filthy lucre\"不义之财\"(廷代尔 1526 年译提多书),lucro 意大利语/西班牙语利润,lucrar,Lohn 是它的德语亲戚。",
    "lucrum (gain) comes from PIE *leh₂u- (to gain, enjoy), shared with Greek apo-lauein (to enjoy), Gothic laun (reward) and German Lohn (wages)! Roman lucrum was profit (lucri causa, for gain); lucrativus profitable (lucrative 1400s), lucre money — filthy lucre (Tyndale's 1526 Titus), Italian and Spanish lucro, lucrar; Lohn is its German cousin."],
  silv: ["silva(森林)词源不明——可能与希腊语 hylē(木材、森林——亚里士多德的\"质料\")同源(存疑,s/h 对应)。罗马人 silva 是林地,Silvanus 是森林之神;silvaticus 野生的——法语 sauvage、意大利语 selvaggio、西班牙语 salvaje、英语 savage(1300 年代——\"林中的\"→野蛮),silvestris 林中的(Sylvester 名字——教皇西尔维斯特),Pennsylvania 是\"佩恩的林地\"(1681),Transylvania 林那边,sylvan 林间的,silviculture 林业,selva 西班牙语/意大利语丛林。",
    "silva (a forest) is of uncertain origin — possibly one word with Greek hylē (timber, forest — Aristotle's matter), though the s/h match is doubted. Roman silva was woodland, Silvanus its god; silvaticus of the woods — French sauvage, Italian selvaggio, Spanish salvaje, English savage (1300s — of the forest → wild), silvestris woodland (the name Sylvester — the popes), Pennsylvania Penn's woods (1681), Transylvania beyond the forest, sylvan, silviculture, Spanish and Italian selva."],
  niv: ["nix(雪,属格 nivis)来自印欧语 *sneigʷʰ-(雪),同根有希腊语 nipha、梵语 sneha(粘)、英语 snow、德语 Schnee、俄语 sneg、爱尔兰语 snechta。罗马人 nix 是雪(阿尔卑斯的 nives);niveus 雪白的,nivalis 雪的(nival),neige 法语雪(nivea→*nivia),neve 意大利语,nieve 西班牙语,Nivea 是 1911 年的\"雪白\"面霜,névé 粒雪(法语),snow 是它的日耳曼亲戚,Sierra Nevada\"雪山\"。",
    "nix (snow, genitive nivis) comes from PIE *sneigʷʰ- (snow), shared with Greek nipha, Sanskrit sneha (stickiness), English snow, German Schnee, Russian sneg, Irish snechta. Roman nix was snow (the Alpine nives); niveus snow-white, nivalis snowy (nival), French neige (from *nivia), Italian neve, Spanish nieve, Nivea the 1911 snow-white cream, névé granular snow, Sierra Nevada the snowy range; snow is the Germanic cousin."],
  arbor: ["arbor(树)来自印欧语 *h₃erdʰ-(长高)存疑,或与 arduus(陡)有关。罗马人 arbor 是树(arbor infelix 处刑的树),arboretum 树园(1830 年代),arboreus 树的(arboreal),arbre 法语,albero 意大利语(r→l 异化),árbol 西班牙语,Arbor Day 植树节(内布拉斯加 1872),arborist 树艺师,arboriculture,arbour(凉亭)与此无关(herbarium 草地→受 arbor 影响)。",
    "arbor (a tree) may come from PIE *h₃erdʰ- (to grow tall), or be kin to arduus (steep). Roman arbor was the tree (the arbor infelix of executions), arboretum a tree-garden (1830s), arboreus of trees (arboreal), French arbre, Italian albero (r dissimilated to l), Spanish árbol, Arbor Day (Nebraska 1872), arborist, arboriculture; arbour (a bower) is unrelated (herbarium, a lawn, reshaped by arbor)."],
  herb: ["herba(草)来自印欧语 *gʰreh₁-(生长、变绿)存疑,同根的可能有英语 grass、grow、green、德语 Gras。罗马人 herba 是草、草药;herbarium 植物标本集(1770 年代),herbal 草药书(1510 年代),herbi-vorus 食草(1660 年代),herbosus,herbe 法语,erba 意大利语,hierba 西班牙语,herbicide 1899,ente en herbe\"青苗状态\"——未成熟,herbalist,Herbert 无关(日耳曼名)。",
    "herba (grass, a herb) may come from PIE *gʰreh₁- (to grow, be green), with English grass, grow, green and German Gras as possible cousins. Roman herba was grass and medicinal plants; herbarium a plant collection (1770s), herbal a book of plants (1510s), herbi-vorus grass-eating (1660s), French herbe, Italian erba, Spanish hierba, herbicide 1899, en herbe budding, herbalist; Herbert is unrelated (Germanic)."],
  bov: ["bos(牛,属格 bovis)来自印欧语 *gʷōus:希腊语 bous、梵语 gauḥ、英语 cow、德语 Kuh、爱尔兰语 bó。拉丁语按音变应是 *vos——bos 是从萨宾语借的(奥斯坎-翁布里亚方言);bovinus 牛的(bovine 1817),bubulcus 牛倌,bubalus 野牛(buffalo 经葡萄牙语 búfalo),bœuf 法语牛肉(beef 1300 年代——诺曼人吃 beef,撒克逊人养 cow),bue 意大利语,buey 西班牙语,Bovril 1886,boustrophedon 是希腊线\"牛耕式\"书写,bucolic 牧牛的(希腊语 boukolos),butter 是 bou-tyron 牛奶酪,hecatomb 百牛祭。",
    "bos (an ox, genitive bovis) is Indo-European *gʷōus: Greek bous, Sanskrit gauḥ, English cow, German Kuh, Irish bó. By Latin sound-law it should be *vos — bos was borrowed from Sabine (Osco-Umbrian); bovinus of oxen (bovine 1817), bubulcus a ploughman, bubalus a wild ox (buffalo via Portuguese búfalo), French bœuf (beef 1300s — the Normans ate beef, the Saxons herded cows), Italian bue, Spanish buey, Bovril 1886, boustrophedon the Greek line's ox-turning script, bucolic (boukolos, a cowherd), butter bou-tyron, cow-cheese, hecatomb the sacrifice of a hundred oxen."],
  serp: ["serpere(爬)来自印欧语 *serp-(爬),同根有希腊语 herpein(爬——herpes 疱疹是\"爬行的\"、herpetology 爬行动物学)、梵语 sarpati(爬)、sarpa(蛇)。罗马人 serpens 是\"爬行者\"——蛇(serpent 1300 年代;伊甸园的蛇),serpentinus 蛇形(serpentine 蛇纹石),Serpens 巨蛇座,serpe 意大利语,sierpe 西班牙语(常用 serpiente),serpent 法语,herpes 是希腊线的\"爬\",surreptitious 是 sub-repere 偷偷爬(repere 是另一个爬),reptile 也是 repere。",
    "serpere (to creep) comes from PIE *serp- (to crawl), shared with Greek herpein (to creep — herpes is the creeping rash, herpetology the study of reptiles), Sanskrit sarpati (creeps), sarpa (a snake). Roman serpens was the creeper — a snake (serpent 1300s; the serpent of Eden), serpentinus snake-like (serpentine the rock), Serpens the constellation, Italian serpe, Spanish sierpe (usually serpiente), French serpent; surreptitious is sub-repere, to creep under (repere, another creeping), and reptile is repere too."],
  unct: ["unguere(涂油,分词 unctus)来自印欧语 *h₃engʷ-(涂),同根有梵语 anakti(涂)、古普鲁士语 anctan(黄油)、古爱尔兰语 imb(黄油)、德语 Anke(黄油,方言)。罗马人 unguere 沐浴后涂油;unguentum 软膏(unguent、ointment 经法语 oignement),unctio 涂油——教会的傅油礼(unction;extreme unction 终傅),unctuosus 油腻的(unctuous 1380 年代物理的\"油\",1740 年代转为\"油滑\"),anoint 是 in-unguere 经法语 enoint(1300 年代;the Anointed 受膏者 = Messiah = Christos!),ungüento,unguis 指甲无关。",
    "unguere (to anoint, participle unctus) comes from PIE *h₃engʷ- (to smear), shared with Sanskrit anakti (anoints), Old Prussian anctan (butter), Old Irish imb (butter), dialect German Anke (butter). Romans unguere after the bath; unguentum a salve (unguent, ointment through French oignement), unctio anointing — the Church's rite (unction; extreme unction), unctuosus oily (unctuous physically in the 1380s, smarmy by the 1740s), anoint in-unguere through French enoint (1300s; the Anointed is the Messiah, the Christos!), Spanish ungüento; unguis (a nail) is unrelated."],
  aur: ["aurum(金)来自印欧语 *h₂ews-(发光、黎明),与 aurora(黎明)、英语 east、德语 Ost 同根——金是\"发光的\";萨宾语 ausom,立陶宛语 auksas,托斯卡语 ausom。罗马的 aureus 是奥古斯都的金币;auratus 镀金的,auri-fer 产金的(auriferous),or 法语金(orfèvre 金匠,Or 是纹章的金),oro 意大利语/西班牙语,Au 元素符号,aureole 光环(aureola corona 金冠),oriole 黄鹂(aureolus 金色的),ormolu 磨金,El Dorado 镀金者,auréole,aurora 黎明,Australia 无关(auster 南风)。",
    "aurum (gold) comes from PIE *h₂ews- (to shine, dawn), kin to aurora (dawn), English east, German Ost — gold is the shining one; Sabine ausom, Lithuanian auksas, Tocharian wäs. The Roman aureus was Augustus' gold coin; auratus gilded, auri-fer gold-bearing (auriferous), French or (orfèvre a goldsmith; Or is heraldry's gold), Italian and Spanish oro, the symbol Au, aureole the halo (aureola corona, golden crown), oriole the golden bird (aureolus), ormolu ground gold, El Dorado the gilded one; Australia is unrelated (auster, the south wind)."],
  hor2: ["hora(小时)是拉丁语借希腊语 hōra(季节、时辰),印欧语 *yeh₁-(年、季),同根有英语 year、德语 Jahr、阿维斯陀语 yārə(年)。希腊的 Hōrai 是时序女神;罗马从前 3 世纪用日晷把白昼分十二 horae(冬夏时长不等!);horarium 时间表,horologium 报时器(法语 horloge 钟,意大利语 orologio 手表),hour 经法语 heure(1200 年代;h 不发音),ora 意大利语,hora 西班牙语,horoscope 是 hōro-skopos\"看出生时辰\",horary,year 是它的日耳曼亲戚。",
    "hora (an hour) is Latin from Greek hōra (a season, a time of day), PIE *yeh₁- (year, season), shared with English year, German Jahr, Avestan yārə (a year). The Greek Hōrai were the goddesses of the seasons; from the 3rd century BC Romans cut daylight into twelve horae by sundial (longer in summer, shorter in winter!); horarium a timetable, horologium a time-teller (French horloge a clock, Italian orologio a watch), hour through French heure (1200s; the h silent), Italian ora, Spanish hora, horoscope hōro-skopos, the look at the birth-hour, horary; year is the Germanic cousin."],
  re2: ["res(事物、财产、事务)来自印欧语 *reh₁-(财产),同根有梵语 rayi(财富)、阿维斯陀语 rāy-。罗马的 res 无所不包:res publica 公共事务——共和国(republic),res privata 私产,res gestae 功业(奥古斯都的《功业录》),res iudicata 已决事项,in medias res 直入事情中央(贺拉斯),rem acu tetigisti 一针见血;realis 事物的(real 1400 年代经法语;realism 1790 年代,reality 1540 年代),realize 1610 年代,realty 不动产,republic,rebus 画谜(\"用东西\"表示),rien 法语\"什么也没有\"是 rem!,Real Madrid 的 real 是 regalis 王家(另一个词)。",
    "res (a thing, property, an affair) comes from PIE *reh₁- (property), shared with Sanskrit rayi (wealth), Avestan rāy-. Roman res covered everything: res publica the public affair — the republic, res privata private property, res gestae deeds (Augustus' Res Gestae), res iudicata a matter decided, in medias res into the middle of things (Horace), rem acu tetigisti you have touched the point; realis of things (real 1400s via French; realism 1790s, reality 1540s), realize 1610s, realty, rebus a puzzle in things, French rien nothing is rem!; the real of Real Madrid is regalis, royal — another word."],
  parv: ["parvus(小)来自印欧语 *peh₂u-(小、少),同根有 paucus、pauper、puer、希腊语 pauros(少)、英语 few。罗马人 parvus 小(比较级 minor、最高级 minimus 来自另一个词);parvulus 幼小的(parvulus 幼童;Jesus 说 sinite parvulos\"让小孩子到我这里来\"),parvitas 渺小,parvi-ficare,Parvati 无关(梵语),parvenu 是法语\"到达者\"(pervenire)无关,parvo 意大利语,parvovirus 是\"小病毒\"(1965),parvis 教堂前庭是 paradisus 无关。",
    "parvus (small) comes from PIE *peh₂u- (small, few), kin to paucus, pauper, puer, Greek pauros (few), English few. Roman parvus was small (its comparative minor and superlative minimus come from another word); parvulus a little child (sinite parvulos, suffer the little children), parvitas smallness, Italian parvo, parvovirus the small virus (1965); parvenu (pervenire, to arrive) and parvis (paradisus) are unrelated."],
  felic: ["felix(幸运、多产)来自印欧语 *dʰeh₁(i)-(吸吮、哺乳),同根有 fecundus(多产)、fetus(胎)、femina(女人——\"哺乳者\")、filius(儿子——\"吃奶的\")、希腊语 thēlē(乳头)、英语 无。罗马人 felix 先是\"结果多\"的树,后是幸运的人(苏拉自称 Felix;Arabia Felix 富庶的阿拉伯);felicitas 幸福——Felicitas 是有神庙的女神,felicity 1370 年代,felicitare 祝福(felicitate),Felix 名字,feliz 西班牙语,felice 意大利语,infelix 不幸的,felicific 功利主义的\"致福\"。",
    "felix (fortunate, fruitful) comes from PIE *dʰeh₁(i)- (to suckle), kin to fecundus (fruitful), fetus, femina (a woman — the suckler), filius (a son — the suckling), Greek thēlē (a nipple). Roman felix was first a fruitful tree, then a lucky man (Sulla styled himself Felix; Arabia Felix the rich Arabia); felicitas happiness — Felicitas a goddess with temples, felicity 1370s, felicitare to wish joy (felicitate), Felix the name, Spanish feliz, Italian felice, infelix unlucky, felicific in the utilitarians' calculus."],
  gaud: ["gaudere(高兴,分词 gavisus)来自印欧语 *geh₂u-(高兴),同根有希腊语 gēthein(高兴)、ganos(光彩)。罗马人 gaudere 欢喜;gaudium 喜悦——法语 joie(1200 年代 joy)、意大利语 gioia(也指珠宝!)、西班牙语 gozo,Gaudeamus igitur\"让我们欢乐吧\"是中世纪大学歌,gaudy 是 gaudium 经中古英语——先指\"节日\",后指\"花哨\"(1580 年代;牛津学院的 gaudy 至今是聚餐),enjoy 是 en-joir,rejoice 是 re-joir,jewel 是 jocale(jocus)——不是 gaudium,gaudente 意大利语享乐者。",
    "gaudere (to rejoice, participle gavisus) comes from PIE *geh₂u- (to rejoice), shared with Greek gēthein (to be glad), ganos (brightness). Romans gaudere were glad; gaudium joy — French joie (joy 1200s), Italian gioia (also a jewel!), Spanish gozo, Gaudeamus igitur the medieval students' song, gaudy gaudium in Middle English — a feast-day, then showy (1580s; an Oxford college gaudy is still a dinner), enjoy en-joir, rejoice re-joir; jewel is jocale (jocus), not gaudium."],
  can: ["canis(狗)来自印欧语 *ḱwō(狗,属格 *ḱunós),同根有希腊语 kyōn(cynic 犬儒——\"像狗的\")、梵语 śvan、英语 hound、德语 Hund、爱尔兰语 cú、俄语 无。罗马人 canis 是狗、也是骰子最差的一掷;caninus 犬的(canine 1600 年代),Canis Major/Minor 大犬小犬座,canicula 小狗——天狼星(dog days 是 dies caniculares 天狼星与太阳同升的酷暑!,canicule 法语热浪),chien 法语,cane 意大利语,can 西班牙语(古;perro 是另一个词),kennel 是 canile 狗舍经法语,canaille 贱民(\"狗群\"),Canary Islands 是 Canariae insulae\"狗岛\"——金丝雀因岛得名!",
    "canis (a dog) comes from PIE *ḱwō (dog, genitive *ḱunós), shared with Greek kyōn (a cynic is dog-like), Sanskrit śvan, English hound, German Hund, Irish cú. Roman canis was the dog and the worst throw at dice; caninus of dogs (canine 1600s), Canis Major and Minor, canicula the little dog — Sirius (the dog days are the dies caniculares when Sirius rises with the sun!, French canicule a heatwave), French chien, Italian cane, old Spanish can (perro is another word), kennel canile through French, canaille the rabble (the dog-pack), and the Canary Islands are Canariae insulae, the isles of dogs — the canary is named for the islands!"],
  lup: ["lupus(狼)来自印欧语 *wĺ̥kʷos,同根有希腊语 lykos(lycanthropy 狼人)、梵语 vṛka、英语 wolf、德语 Wolf、俄语 volk;拉丁语按规律应是 *vulpus——lupus 是萨宾借词(像 bos)。母狼 lupa 哺育罗慕路斯(lupa 也是妓女——所以 lupanar 是妓院!),Lupercalia 是二月的牧神节(luperci 狼人祭司),lupinus 狼的——羽扇豆 lupin(\"狼豆\"),lupus 狼疮(1590 年代,啃噬般的溃疡),loup 法语,lupo 意大利语,lobo 西班牙语,Lupin 名,lupine 狼似的,Lycée 无关(Lykeion)。",
    "lupus (a wolf) comes from PIE *wĺ̥kʷos, shared with Greek lykos (lycanthropy), Sanskrit vṛka, English wolf, German Wolf, Russian volk; by Latin sound-law it should be *vulpus — lupus is a Sabine loan, like bos. The she-wolf lupa suckled Romulus (lupa was also a prostitute — hence lupanar, a brothel!), the Lupercalia the February festival of the wolf-priests, lupinus wolfish — the lupin bean, lupus the disease (1590s, a gnawing ulcer), French loup, Italian lupo, Spanish lobo, lupine; Lycée is unrelated (Lykeion)."],
  leo: ["leo(狮子,属格 leonis)是拉丁语借希腊语 leōn,而希腊语可能借自闪米特语(希伯来语 lābī、阿卡德语 labbu)或埃及语 rw——狮子不是欧洲动物。Leo 狮子座、教皇名(利奥一世劝退阿提拉,452 年),leoninus 狮子的(leonine),leopardus 是 leo-pardus\"狮豹\"(古人以为是杂交),lion 经法语(1200 年代),leone 意大利语,león 西班牙语(León 城是 legio 军团,无关!),Leonard 是日耳曼名\"狮子般强壮\"(leo 借入日耳曼语),Löwe 德语也是 leo 借来的,chameleon 是\"地上的狮子\",dandelion 是 dent de lion 狮齿。",
    "leo (a lion, genitive leonis) is Latin from Greek leōn, which Greek probably took from Semitic (Hebrew lābī, Akkadian labbu) or Egyptian rw — the lion is no European beast. Leo the sign and the papal name (Leo I turned Attila back in 452), leoninus (leonine), leopardus leo-pardus, the lion-panther the ancients took for a hybrid, lion through French (1200s), Italian leone, Spanish león (the city León is legio, a legion — unrelated!), Leonard the Germanic name \"lion-strong\", German Löwe also borrowed from leo, chameleon the ground-lion, dandelion dent de lion, lion's tooth."],
  semin: ["semen(种子,属格 seminis)来自 serere(播种,分词 satus),印欧语 *seh₁-(播),同根有英语 sow、seed、德语 säen、Saat、俄语 sejat'、古爱尔兰语 síl。罗马农夫 serere 播种;seminarium 苗圃——seminary 先是苗圃,16 世纪特伦托会议(1563)用它指培养神职人员的\"苗圃\",seminar 是德国大学 19 世纪借回的\"研讨班\",dis-seminare 撒播(disseminate),seminalis 种子的(seminal 1650 年代\"有种子的\"→\"开创性的\"),semen,satio 播种(season 是 satio 经法语 saison——播种时节!),Saturn 是播种之神(存疑),sative 栽培的(Cannabis sativa),sementera 西班牙语播种,semenza 意大利语。",
    "semen (seed, genitive seminis) comes from serere (to sow, participle satus), PIE *seh₁- (to sow), shared with English sow and seed, German säen, Saat, Russian sejat', Old Irish síl. Roman farmers serere seed; seminarium a seed-plot — seminary was a nursery-garden before the Council of Trent (1563) made it a nursery of priests, seminar the German universities' 19th-century borrowing, dis-seminare to scatter seed (disseminate), seminalis of seed (seminal 1650s, seed-bearing → originating), satio sowing (season is satio through French saison — the sowing-time!), Saturn the sower-god (disputed), sativa cultivated (Cannabis sativa), Spanish sementera, Italian semenza."],
  caus: ["causa(原因、案件)词源不明——可能与 cavere(提防)有关(存疑)。罗马法的 causa 是诉讼案(causam dicere 出庭辩护);causa 也是理由:sine causa 无故,causa finalis 目的因(亚里士多德四因的拉丁译名);causalis(causal),causatio 借口,ac-cusare 归罪(accuse 1300 年代;accusative 宾格是\"被控者\"格——误译希腊语 aitiatikē\"被作用的\"),ex-cusare 开脱(excuse),re-cusare 拒绝(recuse 回避——法官\"拒绝\"审理),cause 经法语 chose——法语 chose 就是\"东西\"!,cosa 意大利语/西班牙语东西,because 是 by cause,causerie 闲谈(法语 causer 说话——\"谈案子\")。",
    "causa (a cause, a case) is of unknown origin — a link to cavere (to beware) is doubtful. In Roman law causa was a lawsuit (causam dicere, to plead); causa was also a reason: sine causa without cause, causa finalis the final cause (the Latin for Aristotle's four causes); causalis (causal), causatio a pretext, ac-cusare to lay a charge (accuse 1300s; the accusative case mistranslates Greek aitiatikē, \"of what is effected\"), ex-cusare to free from blame (excuse), re-cusare to refuse (recuse — a judge refusing to sit), cause through French, and French chose is simply a thing!, Italian and Spanish cosa, because by cause, causerie a chat (French causer, to talk — to plead a case)."],
  vent: ["ventus(风)来自印欧语 *h₂weh₁-(吹)的分词 *h₂weh₁-nt-(吹着的),同根有英语 wind、德语 Wind、哥特语 winds、梵语 vāta、威尔士语 gwynt、希腊语 aēmi(吹)。罗马人 ventus 是风(Ventus 有祭坛);ventilare 扇风、簸谷(ventilate 1520 年代;ventilation),ventosus 多风的、爱吹嘘的,vent 法语风(vent 英语的\"通风口\"是另一路 fendre?混合),vento 意大利语,viento 西班牙语,ventail 头盔面罩,fan 无关,wind 是日耳曼亲戚,weather 也是同根(*we-dhro-),ventiduct 风道,Ventoux 山(风山)。",
    "ventus (wind) is the participle *h₂weh₁-nt- (blowing) of PIE *h₂weh₁- (to blow), shared with English wind, German Wind, Gothic winds, Sanskrit vāta, Welsh gwynt, Greek aēmi (I blow). Roman ventus had altars of its own; ventilare to fan, to winnow (ventilate 1520s; ventilation), ventosus windy, and windbaggy, French vent, Italian vento, Spanish viento, ventail the helmet's breathing-vizor, Mont Ventoux the windy mountain; wind and weather (*we-dhro-) are the Germanic cousins."],
  vet: ["vetus(老,属格 veteris)来自印欧语 *wet-(年),同根有希腊语 etos(年)、梵语 vatsa(一岁的牛犊)、阿尔巴尼亚语 vit(年)、英语 wether(阉羊——\"一岁的\")、德语 Widder。罗马人 vetus 是旧的(与 novus 相对);veteranus 老兵(veteran 1500 年代——服役期满的军团兵得地),veterinarius 与驮兽有关的(vetus→veterinus 驮畜?存疑——veterinary 1640 年代),in-veterare 变老、根深蒂固(inveterate),vieux 法语,vecchio 意大利语(vetulus 小老头),viejo 西班牙语,vetust 古旧的,Vetus Testamentum 旧约。",
    "vetus (old, genitive veteris) comes from PIE *wet- (a year), shared with Greek etos (a year), Sanskrit vatsa (a yearling calf), Albanian vit (a year), English wether (a castrated ram — a yearling), German Widder. Roman vetus was old, the opposite of novus; veteranus an old soldier (veteran 1500s — legionaries got land on discharge), veterinarius of beasts of burden (veterinary 1640s; the link to vetus is disputed), in-veterare to grow old, to take root (inveterate), French vieux, Italian vecchio (vetulus, a little old man), Spanish viejo, vetust, the Vetus Testamentum."],
  rub: ["ruber(红)来自印欧语 *h₁rewdʰ-(红),同根有希腊语 erythros(erythrocyte 红细胞)、梵语 rudhira(血)、英语 red、德语 rot、俄语 rudyj、爱尔兰语 rúad。拉丁语有一族:ruber、rufus(红发——Rufus)、rutilus(金红)、robigo(锈——Robigalia 锈神节);rubrica 红土——用红字写的标题(rubric 1400 年代;礼仪书的红字规程),rubeus 红的(ruby 红宝石),rubescere 变红(rubescent),rubor 脸红,rouge 法语红(化妆),rosso 意大利语(russus),rojo 西班牙语,rubella 风疹(\"小红\"),rubidium 铷(1861,光谱红线),Rubicon 红河,robust 无关(robur 橡木——但也是\"红木\")。",
    "ruber (red) comes from PIE *h₁rewdʰ- (red), shared with Greek erythros (erythrocyte), Sanskrit rudhira (blood), English red, German rot, Russian rudyj, Irish rúad. Latin had a whole family: ruber, rufus (red-haired — Rufus), rutilus (golden red), robigo (rust — the Robigalia festival); rubrica red earth — a heading written in red (rubric 1400s; the red-letter rules of the liturgy), rubeus red (ruby), rubescere to redden (rubescent), rubor a blush, French rouge, Italian rosso (russus), Spanish rojo, rubella the little red rash, rubidium (1861, from its red spectral line), the Rubicon the red river; robust is unrelated (robur, oak — though also red wood)."],
  frig: ["frigus(寒冷)来自印欧语 *srīg-(冷),同根有希腊语 rhigos(寒战)、rhigein(发抖)。罗马人 frigus 是冷、也是冷淡;frigidus 冷的(frigid 1620 年代),frigidarium 浴场的冷水厅,re-frigerare 使凉(refrigerate 1530 年代;refrigerator 1610 年代的\"冷却器\"→1800 年代的冰箱,fridge 1926),froid 法语冷,freddo 意大利语,frío 西班牙语,frigorifique,sang-froid 冷血——镇定,Frigidaire 1918 年商标,frigorie。",
    "frigus (cold) comes from PIE *srīg- (cold), shared with Greek rhigos (a shiver), rhigein (to shiver). Roman frigus was cold and coolness of manner; frigidus cold (frigid 1620s), the frigidarium the baths' cold room, re-frigerare to cool (refrigerate 1530s; refrigerator was a cooling vessel in the 1610s and an icebox by the 1800s, fridge 1926), French froid, Italian freddo, Spanish frío, sang-froid cold blood — composure, Frigidaire the 1918 trade name."],
  fund: ["fundus(底、田产)来自印欧语 *bʰudʰ-(底),同根有希腊语 pythmēn(底)、梵语 budhna、英语 bottom、德语 Boden、爱尔兰语 bonn。罗马的 fundus 是地产(fundus 是田庄——法语 fonds 资金、意大利语 fondo);fundare 打地基(found 1300 年代——建立;foundation),fundamentum 基础(fundamental),pro-fundus 深(profound),fund 是 fundus\"底\"→\"资本\"(1660 年代),foundry 无关(fundere 浇铸),fond 法语底(fond de teint 粉底),fondo 西班牙语/意大利语,funditus 彻底,fondamentale。",
    "fundus (bottom, an estate) comes from PIE *bʰudʰ- (bottom), shared with Greek pythmēn (a base), Sanskrit budhna, English bottom, German Boden, Irish bonn. The Roman fundus was a landed estate (French fonds capital, Italian fondo); fundare to lay a base (found 1300s — to establish; foundation), fundamentum a base (fundamental), pro-fundus deep (profound), fund the bottom → the capital (1660s), French fond (fond de teint foundation cream), Spanish and Italian fondo, funditus from the bottom up; foundry is unrelated (fundere, to pour)."],
  verd: ["viridis(绿)来自 virere(茂盛、变绿),印欧语 *weis-(发芽)存疑。罗马人 viridis 是草木之绿、也是青春;viriditas 生机(希尔德加德的神学关键词),vert 法语绿(verdure 绿意,1300 年代),verde 意大利语/西班牙语,verdant 1580 年代,verdigris 是 vert-de-Grèce 希腊绿(铜绿),Vermont 绿山(法语 vert mont,1777),verdict 无关(vere dictum),Cape Verde 绿角,verdure,vireo 绿鸟,verjuice 酸果汁(vert jus),Verdi 是姓\"绿\"!",
    "viridis (green) comes from virere (to be lush, to green), PIE *weis- (to sprout) possible. Roman viridis was the green of growth and youth; viriditas greenness (Hildegard's theological key-word), French vert (verdure 1300s), Italian and Spanish verde, verdant 1580s, verdigris vert-de-Grèce, Greek green (the copper patina), Vermont the green mountain (French, 1777), Cape Verde, vireo the green bird, verjuice vert jus, and Verdi is the surname Green!; verdict is unrelated (vere dictum)."],
  pauper: ["pauper(穷)是 pau-(少,*peh₂u-)+ -per(获得,parere)——\"得到少的\";同根有 paucus、parvus、puer、英语 few。罗马人 pauper 是穷人(与 dives 相对);paupertas 贫穷,pauper 在英国法是\"贫民\"(pauper 1510 年代——可免诉讼费),pauperism 1815,pauvre 法语穷(1200 年代 poor 经法语 povre),povero 意大利语,pobre 西班牙语,poverty 1170 年代,impoverish,poor 是同一个词,Poverello 阿西西的圣方济各\"小穷人\"。",
    "pauper (poor) is pau- (little, *peh₂u-) + -per (getting, parere) — one who gets little; kin to paucus, parvus, puer, English few. Roman pauper was the poor man, opposite of dives; paupertas poverty, pauper in English law one allowed to sue without fees (1510s), pauperism 1815, French pauvre (poor 1200s through French povre), Italian povero, Spanish pobre, poverty 1170s, impoverish, poor the same word, the Poverello Francis of Assisi, the little poor man."],
  alter: ["alter(两者中的另一个)来自印欧语 *h₂el-(另一)的比较级——同根有 alius(别的)、希腊语 allos(allo-、allegory)、英语 else、other、德语 ander。罗马人 alter 是二者之一(alter ego 另一个我——西塞罗论友谊);alterare 改变(alter 1300 年代),alternus 交替的(alternate、alternative 1580 年代),alter-catio 争吵(altercation),ad-ulterare 掺假——从\"到别处\"来(adulterate;adultery 是 adulterium\"走到别人那里\"!),altruisme 是孔德 1851 年从意大利语 altrui 造的\"利他\",autre 法语,altro 意大利语,otro 西班牙语,alias 是 alius\"另外的名字\",alien 是 alienus 别人的,alibi\"在别处\"。",
    "alter (the other of two) is the comparative of PIE *h₂el- (other) — kin to alius (another), Greek allos (allo-, allegory), English else and other, German ander. Roman alter was one of a pair (alter ego, another self — Cicero on friendship); alterare to change (alter 1300s), alternus by turns (alternate, alternative 1580s), alter-catio a wrangle (altercation), ad-ulterare to corrupt — to go over to another (adulterate; adultery is adulterium, going to another!), altruisme Comte's 1851 coinage from Italian altrui, French autre, Italian altro, Spanish otro, alias another name, alien alienus, belonging to another, alibi elsewhere."],
  ante: ["ante(在前)来自印欧语 *h₂enti(对面、前面),同根有希腊语 anti(对——anti-)、梵语 anti(近)、英语 and(古义\"面对\")、end(古义\"对面\")、德语 ant-(Antwort 回答\"对面的话\")、ent-。罗马人 ante 是空间与时间的\"前\":ante meridiem 午前(a.m.),ante-cedere 走在前(antecedent),ante-cessor 前任(ancestor 经法语 ancestre),ante-natal 产前,anti-cipare 提前拿(anticipate;anti- 是 ante- 的变体),antique 是 anticus\"前面的\"→古老(antiquity),antenna 是 antemna 帆桁(无关?存疑),avant 法语前(avant-garde),antes 西班牙语,anzi 意大利语\"反而\",ancient 是 ante-anus 经法语 ancien,advance 是 ab-ante!",
    "ante (before) comes from PIE *h₂enti (facing, in front), shared with Greek anti (against — anti-), Sanskrit anti (near), English and (once facing) and end (once the opposite side), German ant- (Antwort, an answer — the word facing back), ent-. Roman ante was before in place and time: ante meridiem (a.m.), ante-cedere to go before (antecedent), ante-cessor a predecessor (ancestor via French ancestre), ante-natal, anti-cipare to take beforehand (anticipate; this anti- is a variant of ante-), antique anticus, in front → old (antiquity), French avant (avant-garde), Spanish antes, Italian anzi rather, ancient ante-anus through French ancien, advance ab-ante!"],
  camin: ["camminus(路)是拉丁语从高卢语借的词(凯尔特语 *kamman-,与威尔士语 cam\"步\"同根;印欧语 *ḱeng-(跛行)存疑)。它在晚期拉丁语取代 via,给了罗曼语的\"路\":法语 chemin、意大利语 cammino、西班牙语 camino(Camino de Santiago 朝圣路);caminare 走路(西班牙语 caminar、意大利语 camminare),chimney 是另一个词(caminus 炉,希腊语 kaminos),chemin de fer 铁路(\"铁路\"直译),cheminement 进程,caminante 行人,encaminar 引路,camión 卡车无关。",
    "camminus (a road) is a Latin loan from Gaulish (Celtic *kamman-, kin to Welsh cam, a step). In late Latin it displaced via and became the Romance road: French chemin, Italian cammino, Spanish camino (the Camino de Santiago), caminare to walk (Spanish caminar, Italian camminare), chemin de fer the iron road, cheminement a progression, caminante a wayfarer, encaminar to guide; chimney is another word (caminus, a furnace, Greek kaminos)."],
  pont: ["pons(桥,属格 pontis)来自印欧语 *pent-(路、走过),同根有希腊语 pontos(海——\"过道\"!Pontus 黑海)、patos(路)、梵语 panthāḥ(路)、英语 find(古义\"走过\")、德语 finden、俄语 put'(路)。罗马的 pontifex 是\"造桥者\"——大祭司团(pontifex maximus 大祭司,凯撒任过;教皇沿用 Pontiff 至今!),pons Sublicius 木桩桥(贺拉提乌斯守桥),pont 法语桥(Pont-Neuf),ponte 意大利语,puente 西班牙语,pontoon 浮桥(ponto 平底船),pontifical,pons Varolii 脑桥,transpontine 河那边的,Pontius 无关(萨姆奈名)。",
    "pons (a bridge, genitive pontis) comes from PIE *pent- (a path, to pass), shared with Greek pontos (the sea — a passage! Pontus the Black Sea), patos (a path), Sanskrit panthāḥ (a road), English find (once to pass over), German finden, Russian put' (a way). Rome's pontifex was the bridge-maker — the priestly college (pontifex maximus, held by Caesar; the Pope is still the Pontiff!), the pons Sublicius the pile-bridge Horatius held, French pont (Pont-Neuf), Italian ponte, Spanish puente, pontoon (ponto, a punt), pontifical, the pons of the brain, transpontine across the river; Pontius is unrelated (Samnite)."],
  femin: ["femina(女人)来自印欧语 *dʰeh₁(i)-(哺乳)——\"哺乳者\";同根有 felix、fetus、filius、fecundus、希腊语 thēlys(雌)、英语 无。罗马人 femina 是女性(与 mulier 妇人并用);femininus 阴性的(feminine 1300 年代先是语法性),feminismus 是 1837 年傅立叶?存疑——féminisme 1872 年法语,feminist 1890 年代,effeminare 使女性化(effeminate),femme 法语女人(femme fatale),femmina 意大利语雌性,hembra 西班牙语雌性(f→h),female 是 femella 小女人经法语 femelle——受 male 影响改拼,feminie。",
    "femina (a woman) comes from PIE *dʰeh₁(i)- (to suckle) — the suckler; kin to felix, fetus, filius, fecundus, Greek thēlys (female). Roman femina was the female (beside mulier, a woman); femininus (feminine 1300s, first a grammatical gender), féminisme French 1872, feminist 1890s, effeminare to make womanish (effeminate), French femme (femme fatale), Italian femmina, Spanish hembra (f → h), female femella, a little woman, through French femelle — respelt to match male."],
  coqu: ["coquere(煮,分词 coctus)来自印欧语 *pekʷ-(煮、熟),同根有希腊语 peptein(消化——pepsin、peptide、dyspepsia)、梵语 pacati(煮)、俄语 peč'、英语 无(cook 是借的);拉丁语的 p...kʷ 同化成 kʷ...kʷ。罗马人 coquus 厨子(cook 古英语 cōc 借自它),coquina 厨房(kitchen 古英语 cycene、德语 Küche、法语 cuisine、意大利语 cucina、西班牙语 cocina 都是 coquina!),bis-coctus 烤两次——饼干(biscuit),prae-cox 早熟(precocious——\"先煮熟\"),con-coquere 一起煮——消化、策划(concoct),de-coquere 煎(decoction),apricot 是 praecoquum 早熟果(经阿拉伯语 al-barqūq!),cuire 法语煮,cocer/cocinar 西班牙语,terracotta 烤土,ricotta 再煮的奶酪。",
    "coquere (to cook, participle coctus) comes from PIE *pekʷ- (to cook, ripen), shared with Greek peptein (to digest — pepsin, peptide, dyspepsia), Sanskrit pacati (cooks), Russian peč'; Latin assimilated p…kʷ to kʷ…kʷ. Roman coquus a cook (Old English cōc was borrowed from it), coquina a kitchen (kitchen, German Küche, French cuisine, Italian cucina, Spanish cocina are all coquina!), bis-coctus twice-baked — biscuit, prae-cox early-ripe (precocious), con-coquere to cook together — to digest, to devise (concoct), de-coquere to boil down (decoction), apricot praecoquum, the early fruit, by way of Arabic al-barqūq!, French cuire, Spanish cocer and cocinar, terracotta baked earth, ricotta re-cooked."],
  manduc: ["manducare(咀嚼、吃)来自 mandere(嚼),印欧语 *mendʰ-(嚼)存疑,同根的可能有希腊语 masasthai(嚼)、mastax(嘴——mastication)、威尔士语 mant(颚)。manducus 是罗马滑稽剧里的\"大嚼\"面具;晚期口语用 manducare 替代 edere(吃),给了法语 manger(1200 年代)、意大利语 mangiare、普罗旺斯语 manjar(西班牙语 manjar 美食;西班牙语\"吃\"用 comer=comedere);mandibula 下颌(mandible),mangeoire 食槽,manger 马槽(耶稣降生的 manger 就是 mangeoire——\"吃的地方\"!),blancmange 白食,mangiatoia,mandicare。",
    "manducare (to chew, to eat) comes from mandere (to chew), PIE *mendʰ- (to chew) possible, with Greek masasthai (to chew), mastax (a mouth — mastication), Welsh mant (a jaw) as candidate cousins. Manducus was the gluttonous mask of Roman farce; late speech used manducare instead of edere and gave French manger (1200s), Italian mangiare, Provençal manjar (Spanish manjar a delicacy; Spanish eats with comer = comedere); mandibula the jaw (mandible), mangeoire a feeding-trough, and a manger — the Nativity's — is mangeoire, the eating-place!, blancmange white food."],
  pluv: ["pluvia(雨)来自 pluere(下雨),印欧语 *plew-(流、漂、洗),同根有希腊语 plein(航行——pleustic)、梵语 plavate(漂)、英语 flow、flood、fleet、float、德语 fließen、Flut、俄语 plyt'(游)。罗马人说 pluit\"下雨了\";Iuppiter Pluvius 是降雨的朱庇特,pluvialis 雨的(pluvial 冰期的雨期),pluviometer 雨量计,pluie 法语雨,pioggia 意大利语,lluvia 西班牙语(pl→ll),pluvious,plover 是 pluviarius\"雨鸟\"(1300 年代经法语),pluma 羽毛无关,impluvium 罗马宅院的接雨池。",
    "pluvia (rain) comes from pluere (to rain), PIE *plew- (to flow, float, wash), shared with Greek plein (to sail), Sanskrit plavate (floats), English flow, flood, fleet, float, German fließen, Flut, Russian plyt' (to swim). Romans said pluit, it rains; Iuppiter Pluvius the rain-sender, pluvialis rainy (a pluvial is a glacial age's rainy period), pluviometer, French pluie, Italian pioggia, Spanish lluvia (pl → ll), pluvious, plover pluviarius, the rain-bird (1300s via French), the impluvium the rain-basin of the Roman house."],
  pav: ["pavere(害怕)来自印欧语 *peh₂u-(打、砍)存疑——被打蒙的恐惧;可能与 pavire(捣实)同根。罗马人 pavor 是惊恐(Pavor 与 Pallor 是战神的随从),pavidus 胆怯的,ex-pavescere 惊慌,pavor 给了法语 peur(1100 年代)、意大利语 paura、西班牙语 pavor(西班牙语常用词 miedo 是 metus);peureux 胆小,pauroso,pavura,impavid 无畏的,peur bleue 极度恐惧。",
    "pavere (to be afraid) may come from PIE *peh₂u- (to strike) — the fear of the stunned; perhaps kin to pavire (to ram down). Roman pavor was panic (Pavor and Pallor rode with Mars), pavidus timid, ex-pavescere to take fright, and pavor gave French peur (1100s), Italian paura, Spanish pavor (Spanish's everyday miedo is metus); peureux fearful, pauroso, impavid fearless, peur bleue blue funk."],
  obliv: ["oblivisci(忘记)是 ob-(过去)+ *lēv-(平滑,levis)——\"抹平\";或来自 *lī-(涂抹)。罗马人 oblivio 是遗忘(oblivion 1390 年代);oblivium 忘川——Lethe 的拉丁名,obliviosus 健忘的(oblivious 1450 年代\"健忘\"→1850 年代\"未察觉\"),oublier 法语忘(oblitare),olvidar 西班牙语(音位换位!),obliare 古意大利语(现代 dimenticare 是 de-mente\"从心里出去\"),oubliette 地牢(\"被遗忘的地方\"),Oblivion。",
    "oblivisci (to forget) is ob- (over) + *lēv- (smooth, levis) — to smooth over; or from *lī- (to smear). Roman oblivio was forgetting (oblivion 1390s); oblivium the river of forgetfulness — Lethe in Latin, obliviosus forgetful (oblivious meant forgetful in the 1450s, unaware by the 1850s), French oublier (oblitare), Spanish olvidar (with metathesis!), old Italian obliare (modern dimenticare is de-mente, out of mind), oubliette the dungeon of the forgotten."],
  barc: ["barca(小船)是晚期拉丁语词,可能是 baris 的指小——baris 是希腊语借埃及语 (科普特语 bari) 的\"尼罗河平底船\"。barca 给了意大利语 barca、西班牙语 barca/barco、法语 barque,bark 英语三桅帆船(1400 年代),barge 是 barica 经法语(1300 年代),embark 上船(1540 年代),disembark,barcarolle 威尼斯船歌(奥芬巴赫),Barcelona 无关(迦太基 Barca 家族),barquentine,barcaiolo 船夫,embarcadero 码头。",
    "barca (a small boat) is late Latin, probably a diminutive of baris — Greek from Egyptian (Coptic bari), a Nile flat-boat. barca gave Italian barca, Spanish barca and barco, French barque, English bark the three-master (1400s), barge barica through French (1300s), embark (1540s), disembark, barcarolle the Venetian boat-song (Offenbach), barquentine, Italian barcaiolo a boatman, embarcadero a landing-stage; Barcelona is unrelated (the Carthaginian Barca family)."],
  luct: ["luctari(摔跤)来自印欧语 *leug-(弯),同根有希腊语 lygos(柳条)、lygizein(弯曲)、英语 lock(卷发)、德语 Locke。罗马人 luctari 是摔跤——用四肢缠斗;luctator 摔跤手,re-luctari 挣扎反抗(reluctant 1660 年代——\"挣扎的\"→\"不情愿\"),ineluctabilis 挣不脱的(ineluctable),lutte 法语斗争(lutte des classes 阶级斗争),lotta 意大利语,lucha 西班牙语(lucha libre 自由式摔跤——墨西哥面具摔跤!),luchador,lottatore。",
    "luctari (to wrestle) comes from PIE *leug- (to bend), shared with Greek lygos (a withy), lygizein (to bend), English lock (of hair), German Locke. Roman luctari was wrestling — limbs entwined; luctator a wrestler, re-luctari to struggle against (reluctant 1660s — struggling → unwilling), ineluctabilis not to be wrestled free of (ineluctable), French lutte (lutte des classes), Italian lotta, Spanish lucha (lucha libre, the masked wrestling of Mexico!), luchador, lottatore."],
  salv: ["salvus(安全、完整)来自印欧语 *solh₂-(完整),同根有 solidus(坚实)、sollus(全)、希腊语 holos(全——holo-)、梵语 sarva(全)、英语 无。罗马人打招呼 Salve!\"祝你安好\";salus 安全、健康(Salus 女神;salutem dicere 致意——salute、salutation),salvare 拯救(基督教用它译希腊语 sōzein:salvation 救恩,Salvator 救主),salubris 有益健康(salubrious),salve 药膏无关(日耳曼),safe 是 salvus 经法语 sauf(1300 年代),save,sage 草无关(salvia 是同根!\"治病的草\"),salvage 救捞,salute,salud 西班牙语\"干杯\"(健康!),salut 法语,salvo 意大利语安全,Salvador,Salvation Army 1878。",
    "salvus (safe, whole) comes from PIE *solh₂- (whole), kin to solidus (solid), sollus (entire), Greek holos (whole — holo-), Sanskrit sarva (all). Romans greeted with Salve!, be well; salus safety, health (the goddess Salus; salutem dicere to greet — salute, salutation), salvare to save (the Church's word for Greek sōzein: salvation, Salvator the Saviour), salubris healthy (salubrious), safe salvus through French sauf (1300s), save, salvia the healing herb — sage!, salvage, Spanish salud cheers (health!), French salut, Italian salvo safe, Salvador, the Salvation Army 1878."],
  plang: ["plangere(捶胸、哀哭,分词 planctus)来自印欧语 *pleh₂k-(打),同根有希腊语 plēssein(打——plēgē 打击、apoplexy 中风、plectrum 拨片)、英语 无(fling 存疑)、拉丁语 plaga(打击——plague 瘟疫!)。罗马哀悼者 plangere 捶胸;planctus 哀歌(中世纪的 planctus 体裁),plaint 申诉(1200 年代经法语 plainte),complain 是 com-plangere(1370 年代),plaintiff 原告(\"申诉的人\"),plaintive 哀怨的,piangere 意大利语哭,plañir 西班牙语哀哭(plañidera 哭丧女),plaindre 法语可怜,plangent 回荡哀鸣的。",
    "plangere (to beat the breast, to lament, participle planctus) comes from PIE *pleh₂k- (to strike), shared with Greek plēssein (to strike — plēgē a blow, apoplexy, plectrum), Latin plaga (a blow — plague!). Roman mourners plangere beat their breasts; planctus a lament (the medieval genre), plaint (1200s through French plainte), complain com-plangere (1370s), plaintiff the one who complains, plaintive, Italian piangere to weep, Spanish plañir (the plañidera, a hired mourner), French plaindre to pity, plangent."],
  cort: ["cohors(围栏、步兵大队,属格 cohortis)是 co-+hortus(园——围地);印欧语 *ǵʰer-(围),同根有希腊语 khortos(牧场)、英语 yard、garden、德语 Garten。罗马农庄的 cohors 是畜栏,军团的 cohors 是六百人(十个大队一军团,马略改革),cohors praetoria 禁卫军;cohort 1480 年代(统计学的\"同龄群\"1940 年代),晚期拉丁语 cortem 是院子——法语 cour(宫廷!cour 是国王的院子)、意大利语 corte、西班牙语 corte(Cortes 议会),court 1200 年代(法庭、球场都是\"院子\"),courtesy 宫廷礼,courtier,cortège 随从,curtain 无关(cortina),cortile 意大利语庭院,cortejar 求爱。",
    "cohors (an enclosure, a cohort, genitive cohortis) is co- + hortus (a garden — enclosed ground); PIE *ǵʰer- (to enclose), shared with Greek khortos (a pasture), English yard and garden, German Garten. On the farm a cohors was a pen; in the legion six hundred men (ten to a legion after Marius), the cohors praetoria the guard; cohort 1480s (the statistician's age-group 1940s), late Latin cortem a yard — French cour (the court, the king's yard!), Italian corte, Spanish corte (the Cortes parliament), court 1200s (of law and of tennis alike, a yard), courtesy, courtier, cortège, Italian cortile a courtyard, Spanish cortejar to court; curtain is unrelated (cortina)."],
  joc: ["iocus(玩笑)来自印欧语 *yek-(说),同根有翁布里亚语 iuka(祈祷)、德语 无(jehen 古)、威尔士语 iaith(语言)。罗马人 iocus 是笑话(与 ludus 游戏相对);iocari 开玩笑,iocosus 好笑的(jocose),iocularis 逗趣的(jocular),晚期拉丁语 iocare 取代 ludere 成为\"玩\"——法语 jouer、意大利语 giocare、西班牙语 jugar;jeu 法语游戏(jeu de mots 双关),gioco 意大利语,juego 西班牙语,joke 1660 年代(学生俚语),jewel 是 iocale\"玩物\"经法语 joel(1300 年代)!,jongleur 游吟艺人(ioculator),juggler 也是 ioculator,jeopardy 是 jeu parti\"平分的游戏\"——胜负未定(1300 年代)!",
    "iocus (a jest) comes from PIE *yek- (to speak), shared with Umbrian iuka (prayers), Welsh iaith (language). Roman iocus was a joke (as against ludus, play); iocari to jest, iocosus (jocose), iocularis (jocular), and late Latin iocare replaced ludere as the word for play — French jouer, Italian giocare, Spanish jugar; French jeu (jeu de mots a pun), Italian gioco, Spanish juego, joke 1660s (students' slang), jewel iocale, a plaything, through French joel (1300s)!, jongleur ioculator, juggler the same, jeopardy jeu parti, a divided game — an even chance (1300s)!"],
  coup: ["colaphus(耳光)是拉丁语借希腊语 kolaphos(一巴掌),晚期口语 colpus 给了法语 coup(打;1200 年代)、意大利语 colpo、西班牙语 golpe(音位换位)。coup 在法语里从\"一击\"扩展到一切突发动作:coup d'état 政变(1640 年代),coup de grâce 致命一击(仁慈的),coup de foudre 一见钟情(\"雷击\"),coup de théâtre 戏剧性转折,couper 切(\"一刀\";coupé 切掉的——双门车、coupon 切下的票),coupure 删节,colpire 意大利语击中,golpear 西班牙语打,beaucoup\"很多\"是 beau coup 好大一下!,recoup 追回,decoupage 剪纸装饰。",
    "colaphus (a box on the ear) is Latin from Greek kolaphos (a slap); late colloquial colpus gave French coup (a blow; 1200s), Italian colpo, Spanish golpe (with metathesis). In French coup spread from a blow to any sudden stroke: coup d'état (1640s), coup de grâce the merciful finishing stroke, coup de foudre love at first sight (a thunderbolt), coup de théâtre, couper to cut (one stroke; coupé cut short — the two-door car, coupon the cut-off ticket), coupure, Italian colpire to hit, Spanish golpear, beaucoup much is beau coup, a fine stroke!, recoup, decoupage."],
  gouv: ["gubernare(掌舵)是拉丁语借希腊语 kybernan(掌舵)——不明来源,可能是地中海航海词。柏拉图已把掌舵比作治国;罗马人 gubernator 是舵手(governor 经法语 gouverneur,1300 年代),gubernaculum 舵,governare 晚期义\"治理\"——法语 gouverner、意大利语 governare、西班牙语 gobernar;government 1550 年代,gubernatorial 美国州长的(1734),cybernetics 是维纳 1948 年从 kybernētēs\"舵手\"造的\"控制论\"——与 government 同源!,cyber- 由此,gouvernail 舵,governess 女家庭教师。",
    "gubernare (to steer) is Latin from Greek kybernan (to steer) — of unknown, perhaps Mediterranean seafaring, origin. Plato had already likened the helmsman to the statesman; Roman gubernator was the pilot (governor via French gouverneur, 1300s), gubernaculum the rudder, gubernare in late Latin to rule — French gouverner, Italian governare, Spanish gobernar; government 1550s, gubernatorial (American, 1734), and cybernetics is Wiener's 1948 coinage from kybernētēs, the helmsman — one root with government!, cyber- from it, gouvernail a rudder, governess."],
  hero: ["希腊语 hērōs(英雄)可能来自印欧语 *ser-(保护)——与拉丁语 servare(保护)、Hera(存疑)同根。荷马的 hērōs 是史诗里的战士,后指半神(赫拉克勒斯)和受崇拜的死者(英雄祭);拉丁语 heros,英语 hero 1380 年代(先指半神,1660 年代指主角),heroine 1650 年代,heroic,heroin 是拜耳 1898 年的商标(试用者觉得\"英雄般\"),héros 法语,eroe 意大利语,héroe 西班牙语,Heroic Age 英雄时代,antihero 1714。",
    "Greek hērōs (a hero) may come from PIE *ser- (to protect) — kin to Latin servare (to guard) and, disputedly, Hera. Homer's hērōs was any warrior of the epic; later a demigod (Heracles) and a worshipped dead man (the hero-cult); Latin heros, English hero 1380s (a demigod before the 1660s made him the protagonist), heroine 1650s, heroic, heroin Bayer's 1898 trade name (its testers felt heroic), French héros, Italian eroe, Spanish héroe, antihero 1714."],
  dulc: ["dulcis(甜)来自印欧语 *dl̥k-u-(甜),同根有希腊语 glykys(甜——glucose、glycerine;d→g 异常)。罗马人 dulcis 是甜的、可爱的(dulce et decorum est pro patria mori——贺拉斯,欧文的反讽);dulcedo 甜美,dulcis 经法语 doux/douce(1200 年代),dolce 意大利语(dolce vita 甜蜜生活,la dolce far niente,音乐 dolce),dulce 西班牙语(dulce de leche),dulcimer 是 dulce melos 甜歌(乐器),Dulcinea 是堂吉诃德的\"甜姐\",dulcify,billet-doux 情书,edulcorate 去苦。",
    "dulcis (sweet) comes from PIE *dl̥k-u- (sweet), shared with Greek glykys (sweet — glucose, glycerine; the d/g is irregular). Roman dulcis was sweet and dear (dulce et decorum est pro patria mori — Horace, and Owen's bitter echo); dulcedo sweetness, dulcis through French doux/douce (1200s), Italian dolce (la dolce vita, dolce far niente, the musical dolce), Spanish dulce (dulce de leche), dulcimer dulce melos, sweet song, Dulcinea Don Quixote's sweet lady, dulcify, billet-doux, edulcorate."],
  rich: ["法兰克语 *rīki(有权势的)是日耳曼语从凯尔特语借的——凯尔特语 rīx(王,高卢语 Vercingeto-rix)与拉丁语 rex 同出印欧语 *h₃rēǵ-!所以 rich 与 rex、regal 是远亲。日耳曼语 *rīkja 是\"王国\"(德语 Reich、荷兰语 rijk、英语 bishop-ric 主教辖区);\"有权势\"→\"富有\":古英语 rīce、德语 reich,晚期拉丁语借为 *riccus——法语 riche、意大利语 ricco、西班牙语 rico;richesse 法语财富(riches 是它的单数被当复数),enrich,Richard 是\"强大统治者\",Reich 第三帝国,Frederick 是 \"和平-统治者\"。",
    "Frankish *rīki (powerful) was borrowed into Germanic from Celtic — Celtic rīx (a king, Gaulish Vercingeto-rix) shares PIE *h₃rēǵ- with Latin rex! So rich is a distant cousin of rex and regal. Germanic *rīkja was a kingdom (German Reich, Dutch rijk, English bishop-ric); powerful became wealthy: Old English rīce, German reich, and late Latin borrowed *riccus — French riche, Italian ricco, Spanish rico; richesse French wealth (riches is its singular taken for a plural), enrich, Richard the strong ruler, Frederick the peace-ruler."],
  vill: ["villa(农庄)来自 *wīk-slā,印欧语 *weiḱ-(定居、村),同根有 vicus(村——vicinity 附近、vicinus 邻居)、希腊语 oikos(家——economy、ecology)、梵语 veśa(家)、英语 -wick(地名 Warwick)、哥特语 weihs(村)。罗马的 villa 是乡间庄园(villa rustica 农庄、villa urbana 别墅——哈德良离宫);villanus 庄园农民——villain 是 vilain 农民→粗人→恶棍(1300 年代;villein 是农奴),villaticus→village 村(1400 年代),ville 法语城(Ville-franche;-ville 美国地名),villaggio,villa 意大利语/西班牙语,villancico 西班牙圣诞歌(\"村歌\"),vicinity,vicar?否(vicis),Vichy?否。",
    "villa (a farmstead) comes from *wīk-slā, PIE *weiḱ- (to settle; a village), kin to vicus (a village — vicinity, vicinus a neighbour), Greek oikos (a house — economy, ecology), Sanskrit veśa, English -wick (Warwick), Gothic weihs (a village). The Roman villa was a country estate (villa rustica the farm, villa urbana the retreat — Hadrian's), villanus a farm-worker — villain is vilain, peasant → boor → scoundrel (1300s; a villein a serf), villaticus → village (1400s), French ville a town (Villefranche; American -ville), villaggio, Italian and Spanish villa, villancico the Spanish carol (the village song), vicinity."],
  bel: ["bellus(漂亮)是 bonus(好)的指小 *duenolos——\"小好的\";罗马人多用于女人和孩子(西塞罗嫌它轻佻)。晚期拉丁语用它代替 pulcher(美),给了罗曼语:法语 beau/belle(1200 年代;beauty 是 bellitas→beauté),意大利语 bello(Belle Époque 美好年代、bel canto 美声),西班牙语 bello(常用 bonito 也是 bonus!);belle 美人(南方美人),beau 花花公子,embellish 美化,belladonna\"美女\"——颠茄(滴眼放大瞳孔),Bellini,belvedere 美景楼,Annabel,Isabel 无关(伊丽莎白),Belleville,bellezza。",
    "bellus (pretty) is the diminutive *duenolos of bonus (good) — a little good; Romans used it mostly of women and children (Cicero thought it frivolous). Late Latin let it replace pulcher (beautiful) and passed it to Romance: French beau/belle (1200s; beauty is bellitas → beauté), Italian bello (Belle Époque, bel canto), Spanish bello (and the everyday bonito is bonus too!); belle, beau, embellish, belladonna the fair lady — the nightshade whose drops widened pupils, belvedere the fair view, Annabel, Belleville, bellezza."],
  trav: ["tripalium(三桩刑具)是 tri-(三)+ palus(桩):晚期拉丁语的刑架(用来固定牲畜或折磨人);tripaliare 折磨——古法语 travailler 受苦、劳作(1100 年代;法语 travail 至今是\"工作\"!),travail 英语辛劳(1250 年代;产痛),travel 是同一个词——中世纪旅行就是受苦(1300 年代),trabajar 西班牙语工作,trabalhar 葡萄牙语,travagliare 意大利语受苦(意大利语工作用 lavorare),travailleur 工人,travelogue 1903,traveller,entrave 阻碍(桩)。",
    "tripalium (a three-staked frame) is tri- (three) + palus (a stake): a late Latin rack for holding animals or torturing men; tripaliare to torment — Old French travailler to suffer, to toil (1100s; French travail is still simply work!), travail English toil (1250s; labour in childbirth), and travel is the same word — a medieval journey was suffering (1300s), Spanish trabajar to work, Portuguese trabalhar, Italian travagliare to suffer (Italian works with lavorare), travailleur a worker, travelogue 1903, traveller."],
  pays: ["pagus(乡村区)来自 pangere(固定、钉桩)——用界桩划出的区;印欧语 *peh₂ǵ-(固定),同根有 pax、pactum、希腊语 pēgnynai。罗马意大利分 pagi,高卢部落也按 pagus 划分;paganus 是乡下人、也指\"非军人\"(平民)——基督徒(自称 milites Christi 基督的士兵)称非信徒为 pagani\"平民/乡巴佬\"(4 世纪),pagan 1400 年代,paganism,pagensis 本区的——法语 pays 国家、乡土(1100 年代;paysage 风景→pays),paysan 农民(peasant 1400 年代),país 西班牙语,paese 意大利语(paesano 老乡,paesaggio),Pays-Bas 低地国家,dépayser 使离乡,peasantry。",
    "pagus (a country district) comes from pangere (to fix, to drive in stakes) — a district staked out; PIE *peh₂ǵ- (to fasten), kin to pax, pactum, Greek pēgnynai. Roman Italy was divided into pagi, and Gaulish tribes too; paganus was a countryman and also a civilian — Christians, calling themselves milites Christi, named the unconverted pagani, civilians or rustics (4th century), pagan 1400s, paganism, pagensis of the district — French pays a country, a home region (1100s; paysage a landscape), paysan a countryman (peasant 1400s), Spanish país, Italian paese (paesano a fellow-villager, paesaggio), the Pays-Bas, dépayser to uproot, peasantry."],
  schol: ["希腊语 skholē(闲暇)来自 ekhein(持有)——\"留住的时间\";印欧语 *seǵʰ-(持有,与 hexis、scheme 同根)。雅典人的闲暇用来讨论——skholē 成了讲学、学派(亚里士多德的 Peripatetic 学派);拉丁语 schola 是学校(school 古英语 scōl 借自它;德语 Schule、法语 école、意大利语 scuola、西班牙语 escuela),scholasticus 学者——经院哲学(scholastic),scholar 1300 年代,scholarship,scholium 注疏,Schola Cantorum 唱诗学校(格里高利一世),écolier 小学生,escolar,shoal 鱼群无关。",
    "Greek skholē (leisure) comes from ekhein (to hold) — time held back; PIE *seǵʰ- (to hold, kin to hexis and scheme). Athenians spent their leisure in talk — skholē became a lecture and a school of thought (Aristotle's Peripatetics); Latin schola a school (Old English scōl was borrowed from it; German Schule, French école, Italian scuola, Spanish escuela), scholasticus a learned man — the Schoolmen (scholastic), scholar 1300s, scholarship, scholium a marginal note, the Schola Cantorum (Gregory I's singing school), écolier a schoolboy, escolar."],
  camb: ["cambiare(交换)是拉丁语从高卢语借的(凯尔特语 *kamb-,与威尔士语 newid?否——与 cam\"弯\"同根:换=\"转\");阿普列乌斯已用 cambire。晚期拉丁语给了法语 changer(1200 年代 change)、意大利语 cambiare、西班牙语 cambiar;cambium 是中世纪的\"兑换\"(汇票 littera cambii——letter of exchange),也是植物的形成层(1670 年代,\"交换\"营养),cambist 汇兑商,exchange 是 ex-cambiare,interchange,cambio 意大利语汇率,change 零钱,changeling 调换儿,Cambio。",
    "cambiare (to exchange) is a Latin loan from Gaulish (Celtic *kamb-, kin to Welsh cam, crooked — to change is to turn); Apuleius already used cambire. Late Latin gave French changer (change 1200s), Italian cambiare, Spanish cambiar; cambium was the medieval exchange (the littera cambii, a bill of exchange) and then the plant's growth layer (1670s, where nourishment is exchanged), cambist a dealer in bills, exchange ex-cambiare, interchange, Italian cambio the exchange rate, change small coins, changeling."],
  soir: ["serus(晚)来自印欧语 *seh₁-(长、迟)存疑,同根的可能有古爱尔兰语 sír(长久)、梵语 sāyam(傍晚)。罗马人 sero 太晚了(sero venientibus ossa 来晚的只剩骨头);serum(晚上)给了法语 soir(1100 年代;soirée 晚会 1820 年代),意大利语 sera(buona sera),西班牙语无(tarde 是 tardus),serenata 小夜曲是意大利语 sera + sereno 混合(1640 年代),serotine 晚出的(蝙蝠),serotinous 晚熟的,serein 傍晚的细雨,soirée。",
    "serus (late) may come from PIE *seh₁- (long, late), with Old Irish sír (lasting) and Sanskrit sāyam (evening) as possible cousins. Romans said sero, too late (sero venientibus ossa, latecomers get the bones); serum (evening) gave French soir (1100s; soirée 1820s), Italian sera (buona sera), no Spanish heir (tarde is tardus), serenata the evening song, blending sera and sereno (1640s), serotine the late-flying bat, serotinous late-ripening, serein the evening drizzle."],
});
